window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert-math-matrices","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Matrices · Practice","chapter":"Matrices","subject":"Maths","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":1,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Every diagonal matrix is a square matrix.\n(ii) A zero matrix must always be a square matrix.\n(iii) The matrix $[5]$ is simultaneously a row matrix, a column matrix and a scalar matrix.\n(iv) A diagonal matrix may have some of its diagonal elements equal to zero.\n(v) Every scalar matrix is an identity matrix.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a diagonal matrix is defined only for square matrices.\n(ii) Incorrect: $[0, 0]$ is a $1 \\times 2$ zero matrix.\n(iii) Correct: $[5]$ is $1 \\times 1$, so it has one row and one column, and it is diagonal with its single diagonal entry equal to 5.\n(iv) Correct: the definition only requires the non-diagonal entries to be zero; for example, $\\text{diag}(0, 2, 3)$.\n(v) Incorrect: a scalar matrix is the identity only when $k = 1$.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":2,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let A, B and C be square matrices of the same order. Which of the following statements is always correct?","image":"","options":["$AB = BA$","If $AB = O$, then $A = O$ or $B = O$","$(AB)C = A(BC)$","$(A+B)^2 = A^2 + 2AB + B^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: matrix multiplication is not commutative. For example, NCERT shows $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{bmatrix}$ give $AB \\neq BA$.\n(2) Incorrect: two non-zero matrices can have a zero product. NCERT Example 15 shows this.\n(3) Correct: the associative law $(AB)C = A(BC)$ holds whenever both sides are defined.\n(4) Incorrect: $(A+B)^2 = A^2 + AB + BA + B^2$. This equals $A^2 + 2AB + B^2$ only when $AB = BA$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":3,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Type)\n(a) Row matrix\n(b) Column matrix\n(c) Scalar matrix\n(d) Skew symmetric matrix\nList-II (Example)\n(i) $\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$\n(ii) $\\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$\n(iii) $\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 & 7 \\end{bmatrix}$\n(iv) $\\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\end{bmatrix}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) A row matrix has only one row. The 1 × 3 matrix in (iii) fits.\n(b) A column matrix has only one column. The 3 × 1 matrix in (iv) fits.\n(c) A scalar matrix is diagonal with equal diagonal entries. Matrix (ii) has 0 off the diagonal and 5, 5 on it.\n(d) A skew symmetric matrix satisfies A′ = –A. Matrix (i) has zero diagonal and $a_{12} = -a_{21}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":4,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A triangle has vertices $A(2, -1)$, $B(4, 3)$ and $C(-1, 5)$. Its vertices are written as a matrix in which each column holds the coordinates $\\begin{bmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{bmatrix}$ of one vertex. What is the order of this matrix?","image":"","options":["$3 \\times 2$","$2 \\times 3$","$3 \\times 3$","$2 \\times 2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each vertex gives one column with 2 entries ($x$ and $y$), so there are 2 rows.\nThere are three vertices, so there are 3 columns.\nThe matrix is $\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$, of order $2 \\times 3$.\n$3 \\times 2$ would be the order if each vertex were written as a row instead.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":5,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & x \\\\ 2 & 1 & y \\\\ 2 & -2 & z \\end{bmatrix}$ satisfy $A'A = I$, with $x > 0$. Then $x + y + z$ equals","image":"","options":["1","-1","3","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The (i, j) entry of $A'A$ is the dot product of column i and column j of A. Take the columns of 3A: $c_1 = (1, 2, 2)$, $c_2 = (2, 1, -2)$ and $c_3 = (x, y, z)$.\n$A'A = I$ requires $c_3 \\cdot c_1 = 0$, $c_3 \\cdot c_2 = 0$ and $c_3 \\cdot c_3 = 9$:\n$x + 2y + 2z = 0$ and $2x + y - 2z = 0$.\nAdding the two equations gives $3x + 3y = 0$, so $y = -x$. Substituting back gives $z = \\frac{x}{2}$.\nThen $x^2 + x^2 + \\frac{x^2}{4} = 9$, so $x^2 = 4$, and $x = 2$ since $x > 0$.\nSo $(x, y, z) = (2, -2, 1)$ and $x + y + z = 1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":6,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If A and B are square matrices of the same order with AB = O, then either A = O or B = O.\nStatement II: The product of two diagonal matrices of the same order is a diagonal matrix.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. Take $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$. Both are non-zero, yet AB = O (NCERT Example 15).\nStatement II is true. For $i \\neq j$, the $(i,j)$ entry of the product is $\\sum_k a_{ik}b_{kj}$. Every term vanishes, because $a_{ik} \\neq 0$ only when $k = i$ and $b_{kj} \\neq 0$ only when $k = j$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":7,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = [a_{ij}]_{2 \\times 3}$ with $a_{ij} = i + j$, and $B = [b_{jk}]_{3 \\times 2}$ with $b_{jk} = j - k$. If $C = AB = [c_{ik}]$, then $c_{21}$ equals","image":"","options":["11","2","14","20"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$c_{21} = \\sum_{j=1}^{3} a_{2j} b_{j1} = \\sum_{j=1}^{3} (2+j)(j-1)$.\nj = 1 gives $3 \\times 0 = 0$.\nj = 2 gives $4 \\times 1 = 4$.\nj = 3 gives $5 \\times 2 = 10$.\nSo $c_{21} = 0 + 4 + 10 = 14$.\nThe distractors 11 and 2 are the values of $c_{11}$ and $c_{12}$, which come from using the wrong row or column.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":8,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $\\begin{bmatrix} x^2 - 5x & y^2 \\\\ x + y & 3 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} -6 & 4 \\\\ k & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$, where x and y are real, then the sum of all possible values of k is:","image":"","options":["5","9","10","20"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"From the (1,1) entry: $x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0$, so (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0 and x = 2 or 3.\nFrom the (1,2) entry: $y^2 = 4$, so y = 2 or −2.\nThe (2,1) entry gives k = x + y. The possible values are 2 + 2 = 4, 2 − 2 = 0, 3 + 2 = 5 and 3 − 2 = 1.\nThese four values are distinct, and their sum is 4 + 0 + 5 + 1 = 10.\nIgnoring y = −2 would give only 4 + 5 = 9.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":9,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Two $2 \\times 2$ matrices $X$ and $Y$ satisfy $3X + 2Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 7 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $2X - Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$. The sum of all the elements of $X + Y$ is","image":"","options":["6","2","4","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Let $P$ be the first right-hand matrix and $Q$ the second.\nFrom $2X - Y = Q$, we get $Y = 2X - Q$.\nSubstitute into the first equation: $3X + 4X - 2Q = P$, so $7X = P + 2Q = \\begin{bmatrix} 7 & 14 \\\\ 7 & 7 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nSo $X = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nThen $Y = 2X - Q = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nSo $X + Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$, and the sum of its elements is $3 - 1 + 0 + 2 = 4$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":10,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If A is a matrix of order $3 \\times 4$ and the product AB is a matrix of order $3 \\times 5$, then the order of B is","image":"","options":["$5 \\times 4$","$4 \\times 5$","$3 \\times 5$","$4 \\times 3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"If A is $m \\times n$ and B is $n \\times p$, then AB is defined and has order $m \\times p$.\nThe number of rows of B must equal the number of columns of A, so B has 4 rows.\nAB is $3 \\times 5$, so p = 5 and B has 5 columns.\nHence B is $4 \\times 5$. The option $5 \\times 4$ reverses rows and columns.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":11,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about matrix multiplication is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["If A and B are diagonal matrices of the same order, then AB = BA","If AB is defined, BA need not be defined","If AB = O, then either A = O or B = O","$(AB)' = B'A'$ whenever AB is defined"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the product of two diagonal matrices is diagonal, with entries $a_{ii}b_{ii} = b_{ii}a_{ii}$, so they commute.\n(2) Correct: for A of order 2 × 2 and B of order 2 × 3, AB is defined but BA is not.\n(3) Incorrect: $\\begin{bmatrix}0&-1\\\\0&2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3&5\\\\0&0\\end{bmatrix} = O$, although neither factor is the zero matrix.\n(4) Correct: this is the reversal law for transposes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":12,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"For any square matrix $A$ with real entries, which of the following is always a skew-symmetric matrix?","image":"","options":["$A + A'$","$A'A$","$A - A'$","$A^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Use $(A')' = A$ and $(A \\pm B)' = A' \\pm B'$.\n$(A - A')' = A' - A = -(A - A')$, so $A - A'$ is always skew-symmetric.\n$(A + A')' = A' + A$, so $A + A'$ is symmetric.\n$(A'A)' = A'(A')' = A'A$, so $A'A$ is symmetric.\n$A^2$ has no fixed symmetry in general.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":13,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$, AB = O, even though neither A nor B is a zero matrix.\nStatement II: Whenever the product AB is defined, the product BA is also defined.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: Each row of A is [1 1]. Each column of B is either [1, −1] or [−1, 1].\nEvery row-column product is $1 - 1 = 0$, so AB = O. Statement I is true.\nStatement II: Take A of order $2 \\times 3$ and B of order $3 \\times 4$.\nAB is defined, but BA is not, because 4 ≠ 2. Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":14,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$ be written as $A = P + Q$, where $P = \\frac{1}{2}(A+A')$ is symmetric and $Q = \\frac{1}{2}(A-A')$ is skew-symmetric. If $p_{13}$ is the (1, 3) entry of $P$ and $q_{23}$ is the (2, 3) entry of $Q$, then $p_{13} + q_{23}$ equals:","image":"","options":["$6$","$3$","$-1$","$0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The entries of the two parts are $p_{ij} = \\frac{a_{ij}+a_{ji}}{2}$ and $q_{ij} = \\frac{a_{ij}-a_{ji}}{2}$.\n$p_{13} = \\frac{a_{13}+a_{31}}{2} = \\frac{-1+7}{2} = 3$.\n$q_{23} = \\frac{a_{23}-a_{32}}{2} = \\frac{3-(-3)}{2} = 3$.\nSo $p_{13} + q_{23} = 3 + 3 = 6$.\nOption (3) comes from wrongly adding $q_{13} = -4$ to $p_{13}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":15,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A matrix $A$ has order $3 \\times 2$. For which order of a matrix $X$ is the sum $A + X$ defined?","image":"","options":["$2 \\times 3$","$3 \\times 2$","$3 \\times 3$","$2 \\times 2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Two matrices can be added only when they have the same order. Corresponding elements are then added.\nSo $X$ must also have 3 rows and 2 columns, which means $X$ is $3 \\times 2$.\nThe most tempting wrong choice is $2 \\times 3$. It has the same number of elements (6), but its rows and columns are interchanged, so $A + X$ is not defined.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":16,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos\\theta & \\sin\\theta \\\\ -\\sin\\theta & \\cos\\theta \\end{bmatrix}$ with $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{12}$.\nStatement I: The smallest natural number n for which $A^n = -I$ is n = 12.\nStatement II: $A^{24} = I$ and $A^6$ is a skew symmetric matrix.","image":"","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"By induction (as in NCERT), $A^n = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos n\\theta & \\sin n\\theta\\\\ -\\sin n\\theta & \\cos n\\theta\\end{bmatrix}$.\nStatement I: $A^n = -I$ needs cos nθ = –1 and sin nθ = 0, so nθ = π. This gives n = 12 as the smallest value. Statement I is true.\nStatement II: for n = 24, nθ = 2π, so $A^{24} = I$.\nFor n = 6, nθ = π/2, so $A^6 = \\begin{bmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{bmatrix}$, which satisfies $(A^6)' = -A^6$. Statement II is true.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":17,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2&0\\\\0&3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix}5&0\\\\0&-1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nAssertion (A): $AB = BA$.\nReason (R): The product of any two square matrices of the same order is commutative.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First compute both products.\n$AB = \\begin{bmatrix}10&0\\\\0&-3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $BA = \\begin{bmatrix}10&0\\\\0&-3\\end{bmatrix}$, so A is true.\nThis works because diagonal matrices of the same order always commute: their diagonal entries simply multiply.\nR is false. Square matrices of the same order need not commute. For example, $\\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{bmatrix}$ give $AB \\neq BA$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":18,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"For an arbitrary square matrix A with real entries, how many of the following are always symmetric?\n(i) $A + A'$\n(ii) $AA'$\n(iii) $A - A'$\n(iv) $A'A$\n(v) $A^2$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Symmetric: $(A + A')' = A' + A = A + A'$.\n(ii) Symmetric: $(AA')' = (A')'A' = AA'$.\n(iii) Not symmetric: $(A - A')' = -(A - A')$, so it is skew symmetric.\n(iv) Symmetric: $(A'A)' = A'(A')' = A'A$.\n(v) Not always symmetric: $(A^2)' = (A')^2$, which need not equal $A^2$.\nSo 3 expressions are always symmetric.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":19,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"For an arbitrary square matrix $A$ with real entries, how many of the following are always symmetric?\n(i) $A + A'$\n(ii) $AA'$\n(iii) $A - A'$\n(iv) $A'A$\n(v) $A^2$\n(vi) $A^2 - (A')^2$","image":"","options":["$2$","$3$","$4$","$5$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Symmetric: $(A+A')' = A' + A$.\n(ii) Symmetric: $(AA')' = (A')'A' = AA'$.\n(iii) Not symmetric: $(A - A')' = -(A - A')$, so it is skew-symmetric.\n(iv) Symmetric: $(A'A)' = A'A$.\n(v) Not always: $(A^2)' = (A')^2$, which need not equal $A^2$.\n(vi) Not symmetric: its transpose is $(A')^2 - A^2$, the negative of itself, so it is skew-symmetric.\nThree are always symmetric.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":20,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A shop records the sales (in ₹) of two farmers P (row 1) and Q (row 2) for Basmati, Permal and Naura (columns, in that order).\nSeptember: $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 10000 & 20000 & 15000 \\\\ 25000 & 12000 & 18000 \\end{bmatrix}$\nOctober: $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 6000 & 15000 & 10000 \\\\ 20000 & 10000 & 12000 \\end{bmatrix}$\nBoth farmers earn 2% profit on gross sales. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Combined Basmati sales of P in the two months\n(b) Decrease in Naura sales of Q from September to October\n(c) October profit of P on Permal\n(d) Total October profit of Q on all three varieties\nList-II\n(i) ₹ 300\n(ii) ₹ 840\n(iii) ₹ 6000\n(iv) ₹ 16000","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) From $A + B$, entry (1,1) is $10000 + 6000 = 16000$, which matches (iv).\n(b) From $A - B$, entry (2,3) is $18000 - 12000 = 6000$, which matches (iii).\n(c) From $0.02B$, entry (1,2) is $0.02 \\times 15000 = 300$, which matches (i).\n(d) Row 2 of $0.02B$ sums to $0.02 \\times (20000 + 10000 + 12000) = 840$, which matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":21,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If A is a square matrix such that $A^2 = A$, then $(I - A)^3 + 2A$ is equal to","image":"","options":["$I + A$","$I - A$","$I$","$I + 3A$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Expand $(I - A)^2 = I - 2A + A^2$. Since $A^2 = A$, this becomes $I - 2A + A = I - A$.\nStep 2: Then $(I - A)^3 = (I - A)^2(I - A) = (I - A)^2 = I - A$.\nStep 3: So $(I - A)^3 + 2A = I - A + 2A = I + A$.\nOption $I$ is tempting only if the $2A$ term is overlooked.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":22,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If A and B are invertible square matrices of the same order, then $(AB)^{-1}$ equals","image":"","options":["$B^{-1}A^{-1}$","$A^{-1}B^{-1}$","$A^{-1} + B^{-1}$","$BA$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Check $B^{-1}A^{-1}$ on the right of AB.\n$(AB)(B^{-1}A^{-1}) = A(BB^{-1})A^{-1} = AIA^{-1} = AA^{-1} = I$.\nStep 2: Check it on the left.\n$(B^{-1}A^{-1})(AB) = B^{-1}(A^{-1}A)B = B^{-1}B = I$.\nStep 3: Hence $(AB)^{-1} = B^{-1}A^{-1}$. The order of the factors is reversed.\nStep 4: Option (2), $A^{-1}B^{-1}$, keeps the original order. It is wrong because matrix multiplication is not commutative.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":23,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$, which of the following matrices is $A^{-1}$?","image":"","options":["$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: Test each option by checking whether the product with A is I.\nStep 2: Take $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nAB = [[6−5, −15+15], [2−2, −5+6]] = I.\nStep 3: BA = [[6−5, 10−10], [−3+3, −5+6]] = I.\nSo AB = BA = I, and B = $A^{-1}$.\nStep 4: Option (4) is the negative of B, so the product equals −I, not I.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":24,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"The matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $A^2 - 5A + 7I = O$. Using this relation, $A^{-1}$ equals","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{7}(A - 5I)$","$7(5I - A)$","$\\frac{1}{7}(5I - A)$","$\\frac{1}{5}(A + 7I)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: Rearrange the relation: $5A - A^2 = 7I$.\nStep 2: Factor out A on each side:\n$A(5I - A) = 7I$ and $(5I - A)A = 7I$.\nStep 3: Divide by 7. Then $A \\cdot \\frac{1}{7}(5I - A) = \\frac{1}{7}(5I - A) \\cdot A = I$.\nSo $A^{-1} = \\frac{1}{7}(5I - A)$.\nStep 4: Check: $5I - A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$. Multiplying A by this matrix gives $\\begin{bmatrix} 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 \\end{bmatrix}$ = 7I.\nStep 5: Option (1) has the wrong sign; it would give −I.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":25,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) $[0 \\;\\; 0 \\;\\; 0]$\n(B) $\\begin{bmatrix} -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{bmatrix}$\n(C) $\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$\n(D) $\\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\\\ 7 \\end{bmatrix}$\nList-II\n(I) Column matrix\n(II) Diagonal matrix that is not scalar\n(III) Scalar matrix that is not an identity matrix\n(IV) Null matrix","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) All its entries are zero, so it is a null matrix. Being $1 \\times 3$ (not square), it cannot be scalar: A-IV.\n(B) It is diagonal with equal diagonal entries $k = -2 \\ne 1$, so it is scalar but not identity: B-III.\n(C) It is diagonal, but its diagonal entries 1 and 5 are unequal, so it is not scalar: C-II.\n(D) It has a single column, so it is a $2 \\times 1$ column matrix: D-I.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":26,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Matrices $X$ and $Y$ satisfy $2X + 3Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $3X - 2Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -5 \\end{bmatrix}$. Then $Y$ equals","image":"","options":["$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 13 & 0 \\\\ 26 & 13 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Call the right-hand sides P and Q. Then $3P = 6X + 9Y$ and $2Q = 6X - 4Y$.\nSubtracting eliminates X: $3P - 2Q = 13Y$.\n$3P - 2Q = \\begin{bmatrix} 15-2 & 12-12 \\\\ 18+8 & 3+10 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 13 & 0 \\\\ 26 & 13 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nSo $Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nSimilarly $13X = 2P + 3Q$ gives $X = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$, which is option (1), a trap.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":27,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"For $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$, it is given that $A^3 = pA + qI$ for some scalars $p, q$. The value of $p + q$ is","image":"","options":["1","13","$-1$","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Compute $A^2 = \\begin{bmatrix} 16-6 & -12+3 \\\\ 8-2 & -6+1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 10 & -9 \\\\ 6 & -5 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nCompare with $3A - 2I = \\begin{bmatrix} 10 & -9 \\\\ 6 & -5 \\end{bmatrix}$. So $A^2 = 3A - 2I$.\nMultiply by $A$: $A^3 = 3A^2 - 2A = 3(3A - 2I) - 2A = 7A - 6I$.\nSo $p = 7$ and $q = -6$, giving $p + q = 1$.\nCheck: $7A - 6I = \\begin{bmatrix} 22 & -21 \\\\ 14 & -13 \\end{bmatrix}$, which equals $A^2 A$ computed directly.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":28,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = [a_{ij}]_{3 \\times 4}$, where $a_{ij} = |3i - 2j|$. Consider the following statements:\n(i) $a_{23} = 0$\n(ii) The element in the 3rd row and 2nd column of A is 4\n(iii) Exactly one element of A is zero\n(iv) The sum of the elements of the second column of A is 8\n(v) $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i, j \\in \\{1, 2, 3\\}$\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Build the matrix row by row.\nRow 1: $|3-2|, |3-4|, |3-6|, |3-8| = 1, 1, 3, 5$\nRow 2: $4, 2, 0, 2$\nRow 3: $7, 5, 3, 1$\n(i) Correct: $a_{23} = |6 - 6| = 0$.\n(ii) Incorrect: $a_{32} = |9 - 4| = 5$, not 4.\n(iii) Correct: only $a_{23}$ is zero.\n(iv) Correct: the second column is $1, 2, 5$, and $1 + 2 + 5 = 8$.\n(v) Incorrect: $a_{12} = 1$ but $a_{21} = 4$.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":29,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"How many different matrices of order $3 \\times 3$ can be formed if each entry is chosen from the set $\\{0, 1, 2\\}$?","image":"","options":["512","729","19683","27"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A $3 \\times 3$ matrix has $3 \\times 3 = 9$ entries.\nEach entry can be filled independently in 3 ways.\nTotal number of matrices $= 3^9 = 19683$.\n512 $= 2^9$ is the count when entries are only 0 or 1.\n729 $= 3^6$ undercounts the number of entries.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":30,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A$ be a symmetric matrix and $B$ a skew symmetric matrix of the same order. How many of the following matrices are necessarily skew symmetric?\n(i) $AB - BA$\n(ii) $AB + BA$\n(iii) $B^2$\n(iv) $B^3$\n(v) $ABA$\n(vi) $A^2$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use $A' = A$, $B' = -B$ and $(XY)' = Y'X'$.\n(i) $(AB-BA)' = B'A' - A'B' = -BA + AB$, which is the matrix itself, so it is symmetric.\n(ii) $(AB+BA)' = -BA - AB = -(AB+BA)$, so it is skew symmetric.\n(iii) $(B^2)' = (-B)(-B) = B^2$, so it is symmetric.\n(iv) $(B^3)' = (-B)^3 = -B^3$, so it is skew symmetric.\n(v) $(ABA)' = A'B'A' = A(-B)A = -ABA$, so it is skew symmetric.\n(vi) $(A^2)' = A^2$, so it is symmetric.\nThree matrices are skew symmetric: (ii), (iv) and (v).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":31,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A matrix whose number of elements is a prime number $p$ has exactly two possible orders.\nStatement II: A matrix of order $m \\times n$ has $m + n$ elements.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: we need $mn = p$. Since $p$ is prime, the only factorisations are $1 \\times p$ and $p \\times 1$, so there are exactly two orders. True.\nStatement II: an $m \\times n$ matrix has $m$ rows, each containing $n$ elements, so it has $mn$ elements, not $m + n$. False.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":32,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $A^2 = kA - 3I$, where $I$ is the identity matrix of order 2, then the value of $k$ is","image":"","options":["4","3","5","$-4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Compute $A^2$: $\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nWrite out the right side: $kA - 3I = \\begin{bmatrix} 2k-3 & k \\\\ k & 2k-3 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nEquate corresponding elements. The (1,2) entries give $k = 4$.\nCheck with the (1,1) entries: $2(4) - 3 = 5$, which matches.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":33,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$, $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $C = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$. The matrix D such that $CD - AB = O$ is","image":"","options":["$\\begin{bmatrix} 6 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} -4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} -4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First, AB = [[2, 1], [4+1, 2+1]] = [[2, 1], [5, 3]].\nLet D = [[a, b], [c, d]]. Then CD = [[a + 2c, b + 2d], [a + 3c, b + 3d]].\nEquate CD = AB, first column: a + 2c = 2 and a + 3c = 5, so c = 3 and a = −4.\nSecond column: b + 2d = 1 and b + 3d = 3, so d = 2 and b = −3.\nSo D = [[−4, −3], [3, 2]].\nOption (1) is the trap: it comes from using BA instead of AB.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":34,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A = [a_{ij}]$ is constructed using $a_{ij} = \\frac{(2i - j)^2}{2}$. Find the sum of all the elements of A.","image":"","options":["8","9.5","7","10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Row 1 ($i = 1$): $a_{11} = \\frac{1^2}{2} = 0.5$, $a_{12} = \\frac{0^2}{2} = 0$, $a_{13} = \\frac{(-1)^2}{2} = 0.5$\nRow 2 ($i = 2$): $a_{21} = \\frac{3^2}{2} = 4.5$, $a_{22} = \\frac{2^2}{2} = 2$, $a_{23} = \\frac{1^2}{2} = 0.5$\nSum $= 0.5 + 0 + 0.5 + 4.5 + 2 + 0.5 = 8$\nA common slip is using $(2j - i)$ instead of $(2i - j)$, which changes the entries in row 2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":35,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos\\theta & \\sin\\theta\\\\ -\\sin\\theta & \\cos\\theta\\end{bmatrix}$ with $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Then $A^6$ equals","image":"","options":["$I$","$-I$","$A$","$O$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"By induction (NCERT), $A^n=\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos n\\theta & \\sin n\\theta\\\\ -\\sin n\\theta & \\cos n\\theta\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWith $n=6$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$, we get $n\\theta=\\pi$.\nSo $A^6=\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos\\pi&\\sin\\pi\\\\-\\sin\\pi&\\cos\\pi\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{bmatrix}=-I$.\n$I$ would need $n\\theta=2\\pi$, that is $A^{12}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":36,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(P) $\\begin{bmatrix}3&0\\\\0&3\\end{bmatrix}$\n(Q) $\\begin{bmatrix}0&2\\\\-2&0\\end{bmatrix}$\n(R) $\\begin{bmatrix}1&4\\\\4&5\\end{bmatrix}$\n(S) $\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix}$\nList-II\n(i) Skew symmetric matrix\n(ii) Column matrix\n(iii) Scalar matrix\n(iv) Symmetric matrix that is not diagonal","image":"","options":["P-iii, Q-iv, R-i, S-ii","P-iii, Q-i, R-iv, S-ii","P-iv, Q-i, R-iii, S-ii","P-ii, Q-i, R-iv, S-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(P) The off-diagonal elements are 0 and the diagonal elements are equal (3), so P is a scalar matrix: P-iii.\n(Q) The diagonal is zero and $a_{12} = -a_{21}$, so Q' = -Q and Q is skew symmetric: Q-i.\n(R) Here $a_{12} = a_{21} = 4$, so R' = R. Its off-diagonal entries are non-zero, so it is not diagonal: R-iv.\n(S) S has order 2 × 1, so it is a column matrix: S-ii.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":37,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos\\theta & \\sin\\theta\\\\-\\sin\\theta & \\cos\\theta\\end{bmatrix}$ with $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{12}$.\nStatement I: $A^6$ is a skew symmetric matrix.\nStatement II: $A^{12} = I$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"By induction (NCERT Example 23), $A^n = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos n\\theta & \\sin n\\theta\\\\-\\sin n\\theta & \\cos n\\theta\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFor n = 6, $6\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, so $A^6 = \\begin{bmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{bmatrix}$. Its transpose is $-A^6$, so it is skew symmetric. Statement I is true.\nFor n = 12, $12\\theta = \\pi$, so $A^{12} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{bmatrix} = -I \\neq I$. Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":38,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A matrix has exactly 12 elements. How many different orders can this matrix have?","image":"","options":["3","4","6","12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"An m × n matrix has mn elements, so we need ordered pairs of natural numbers (m, n) with mn = 12.\nThe divisors of 12 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12. Each divisor m gives exactly one n = 12/m.\nSo the ordered pairs are (1,12), (12,1), (2,6), (6,2), (3,4) and (4,3), which is 6 orders.\nThe order matters, so 3 × 4 and 4 × 3 are counted separately.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":39,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$. If $C = AB = [c_{ik}]$, then $c_{23}$ equals","image":"","options":["14","18","10","22"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use $c_{ik} = \\sum_j a_{ij} b_{jk}$. Here $c_{23}$ comes from row 2 of A and column 3 of B.\nRow 2 of A is (-1, 3, 4) and column 3 of B is (-1, 3, 2).\n$c_{23} = (-1)(-1) + 3(3) + 4(2)$.\nThis is $1 + 9 + 8 = 18$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":40,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"How many different orders are possible for a matrix having exactly 36 elements?","image":"","options":["9","6","8","12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"An m × n matrix has mn elements, so we need ordered pairs of natural numbers (m, n) with mn = 36.\nThe divisors of 36 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18 and 36.\nEach divisor m fixes n = 36/m, so there are 9 ordered pairs. Note that 6 × 6 is counted only once.\nSo the number of possible orders is 9.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":41,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Every diagonal element of a skew-symmetric matrix is zero.\nReason (R): In a skew-symmetric matrix $a_{ji} = -a_{ij}$ for all $i, j$, and putting $i = j$ gives $2a_{ii} = 0$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT shows that all diagonal entries of a skew-symmetric matrix vanish.\nR is true: $A' = -A$ means $a_{ji} = -a_{ij}$ for every $i, j$.\nPutting $i = j$ gives $a_{ii} = -a_{ii}$, so $2a_{ii} = 0$ and $a_{ii} = 0$.\nThis is exactly how A is derived, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":42,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $\\begin{bmatrix} x+2y & 3 \\\\ 4 & x-y \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 8 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$, then the value of $xy$ is:","image":"","options":["6","8","12","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: two equal 2 × 2 matrices, so their corresponding entries are equal.\nEquating entries: $x+2y=8$ and $x-y=2$.\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives $3y=6$, so $y=2$. Then $x=2+y=4$.\nSo $xy=4\\times 2=8$.\nOption 6 is the value of $x+y$, not the product $xy$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":43,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For every square matrix A, the matrix $AA'$ is symmetric.\nReason (R): For matrices of suitable orders, $(AB)' = B'A'$ and $(A')' = A$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: both are standard properties of the transpose stated in NCERT.\nApply them to $AA'$: $(AA')' = (A')'A' = AA'$.\nSince $(AA')' = AA'$, the matrix $AA'$ is symmetric, so A is true.\nThe proof of A uses exactly the two results stated in R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":44,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = [a_{ij}]$ be a $2 \\times 3$ matrix with $a_{ij} = 2i - j$. What is the element in the 3rd row and 1st column of $A'$?","image":"","options":["$-1$","$1$","$4$","$0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The transpose is defined by $A' = [a_{ji}]$. So the (3, 1)th element of $A'$ equals the (1, 3)th element of A.\nSubstituting i = 1, j = 3: $a_{13} = 2(1) - 3 = -1$.\nThe value 1 comes from $a_{23}$, which wrongly uses i = 2. The element $a_{31}$ does not exist, since A has only 2 rows.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":45,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A$ and $B$ be symmetric matrices of the same order.\nStatement I: $AB$ is always a symmetric matrix.\nStatement II: $AB - BA$ is always a skew-symmetric matrix.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Since $A$ and $B$ are symmetric, $A' = A$ and $B' = B$.\nStatement I: $(AB)' = B'A' = BA$. So $AB$ is symmetric only when $AB = BA$, which need not hold. False.\nStatement II: $(AB - BA)' = B'A' - A'B' = BA - AB = -(AB - BA)$. So it is always skew-symmetric. True.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":46,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let A, B and C be square matrices of the same order.\nStatement I: $(A + B)^2 = A^2 + 2AB + B^2$ for all such A and B.\nStatement II: $(A + B)C = AC + BC$ for all such A, B and C.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Expand using the distributive laws: (A + B)² = (A + B)(A + B) = A² + AB + BA + B².\nThis equals A² + 2AB + B² only if AB = BA, which is not always true. So Statement I is false.\nStatement II is the distributive law (A + B)C = AC + BC. It holds whenever the products are defined, so it is true.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":47,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A manufacturer sells products x, y, z in two markets. Annual sales (in units) are:\nMarket I: 2000, 4000, 1000\nMarket II: 3000, 1000, 5000\nThe unit selling prices of x, y, z are ₹3, ₹2 and ₹4. The unit costs are ₹2, ₹1.50 and ₹2.50. Using matrix multiplication, what is the total gross profit from both markets?","image":"","options":["₹16500","₹49000","₹11000","₹32500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Profit per unit = price − cost, giving the column matrix [1, 0.5, 1.5].\nMultiply the sales matrix (2×3) by this column (3×1).\nMarket I: $2000(1)+4000(0.5)+1000(1.5)=5500$.\nMarket II: $3000(1)+1000(0.5)+5000(1.5)=11000$.\nTotal gross profit = 5500 + 11000 = ₹16500.\n₹49000 is the total revenue, and ₹32500 is the total cost.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":48,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1\\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $A^2-5A+7I=O$. Using this relation, $A^3$ can be written as","image":"","options":["$18A-35I$","$25A-35I$","$18A-7I$","$32A-35I$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Check the relation: $A^2=\\begin{bmatrix}8&5\\\\-5&3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $5A-7I=\\begin{bmatrix}8&5\\\\-5&3\\end{bmatrix}$, so $A^2=5A-7I$.\nMultiply both sides by $A$: $A^3=5A^2-7A$.\nSubstitute $A^2$ again: $A^3=5(5A-7I)-7A=25A-35I-7A=18A-35I$.\nOption (2) forgets to subtract the $7A$ term.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":49,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ x & 2 & y \\end{bmatrix}$, where x and y are real numbers. If $A'A = I_3$, then the value of $x + y$ is","image":"","options":["$-3$","$3$","$-1$","$1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key idea: the (i, j)th entry of $A'A$ is the dot product of column i and column j of A. So $A'A = I$ means distinct columns have dot product 0.\nColumns (dropping the common factor $\\frac{1}{3}$): $c_1 = (1, 2, x)$, $c_2 = (2, 1, 2)$, $c_3 = (2, -2, y)$.\n$c_1 \\cdot c_2 = 2 + 2 + 2x = 0$, so $x = -2$.\n$c_2 \\cdot c_3 = 4 - 2 + 2y = 0$, so $y = -1$.\nCheck: $c_1 \\cdot c_3 = 2 - 4 + xy = -2 + 2 = 0$. Each column has squared length 9, so after the factor $\\frac{1}{9}$ the diagonal entries are 1.\nSo $x + y = -3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":50,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"For matrices A and B of suitable orders and a scalar k, which of the following is INCORRECT in general?","image":"","options":["$(A')' = A$","$(kA)' = kA'$","$(A + B)' = A' + B'$","$(AB)' = A'B'$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: transposing twice returns the original matrix.\n(2) Correct: a scalar factor passes through the transpose.\n(3) Correct: the transpose distributes over a sum.\n(4) Incorrect: the true rule reverses the order, $(AB)' = B'A'$. If A is $m \\times n$ and B is $n \\times p$, then $A'$ is $n \\times m$ and $B'$ is $p \\times n$. So $A'B'$ is not even defined unless p = m.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":51,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"$A$ and $B$ are square matrices of the same order. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) $B'AB$, where $A$ is symmetric\n(b) $B'AB$, where $A$ is skew-symmetric\n(c) $AB - BA$, where $A$ and $B$ are both symmetric\n(d) $AB + BA$, where $A$ and $B$ are both symmetric\nList-II\n(P) always symmetric\n(Q) always skew-symmetric","image":"","options":["a-P, b-Q, c-Q, d-P","a-P, b-Q, c-P, d-Q","a-Q, b-P, c-Q, d-P","a-P, b-P, c-Q, d-Q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $(XYZ)' = Z'Y'X'$.\n(a) $(B'AB)' = B'A'B = B'AB$ since $A' = A$, so it is symmetric (P).\n(b) $(B'AB)' = B'A'B = -B'AB$ since $A' = -A$, so it is skew-symmetric (Q).\n(c) $(AB - BA)' = BA - AB = -(AB - BA)$, so it is skew-symmetric (Q).\n(d) $(AB + BA)' = BA + AB$, so it is symmetric (P).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":52,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{bmatrix}$, then $A'A$ is a $1 \\times 1$ matrix $[c]$. What is the value of c?","image":"","options":["14","6","36","12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is $3 \\times 1$, so $A' = [1\\ 2\\ 3]$ is $1 \\times 3$.\nThe product of a $1 \\times 3$ and a $3 \\times 1$ matrix is $1 \\times 1$.\nWorking it out: $A'A = [1(1) + 2(2) + 3(3)] = [1 + 4 + 9] = [14]$.\nThe value 6 just adds the entries, and 36 squares that sum. Neither is a matrix product.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":53,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix} x + y & y - 2 \\\\ x - 3z & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$ is a scalar matrix, then what is the value of $x + y + z$?","image":"","options":["5","4","6","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"For a scalar matrix, the off-diagonal entries must be 0 and the diagonal entries must be equal.\nOff-diagonal conditions: $y - 2 = 0$ gives $y = 2$, and $x - 3z = 0$.\nDiagonal condition: $x + y = 5$ gives $x = 3$.\nThen $3 - 3z = 0$ gives $z = 1$.\nSo $x + y + z = 3 + 2 + 1 = 6$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":54,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & a & 3\\\\ 2 & b & -1\\\\ c & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is skew-symmetric. The value of $a+b+c$ is","image":"","options":["$-5$","$-1$","$5$","$1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For a skew-symmetric matrix, $a_{ji}=-a_{ij}$ for all $i, j$, and every diagonal entry is 0.\nFrom $a_{12}=-a_{21}$: $a=-2$.\nFrom $a_{22}=0$: $b=0$.\nFrom $a_{31}=-a_{13}$: $c=-3$. As a check, $a_{23}=-1$ and $a_{32}=1$ are consistent.\nSo $a+b+c=-2+0-3=-5$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":55,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Each entry of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix is taken from the set $\\{-1, 0, 1\\}$. How many such matrices are diagonal matrices but NOT scalar matrices?","image":"","options":["27","24","26","21"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Diagonal: the 6 non-diagonal entries must be 0, and each of the 3 diagonal entries can be $-1$, $0$ or $1$. This gives $3^3 = 27$ diagonal matrices.\nScalar: all three diagonal entries must be equal to the same $k \\in \\{-1, 0, 1\\}$, giving 3 matrices. These are $-I$, $O$ and $I$; note that $O$ is also scalar, with $k = 0$.\nRequired count $= 27 - 3 = 24$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":56,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & -1\\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\\\ 3 & -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$ is written as $A=P+Q$, where $P=[p_{ij}]$ is symmetric and $Q=[q_{ij}]$ is skew-symmetric. The value of $p_{13}+q_{23}$ is","image":"","options":["$4$","$-2$","$-1$","$1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: $P=\\frac{1}{2}(A+A')$ and $Q=\\frac{1}{2}(A-A')$, so $p_{ij}=\\frac{a_{ij}+a_{ji}}{2}$ and $q_{ij}=\\frac{a_{ij}-a_{ji}}{2}$.\n$p_{13}=\\frac{-1+3}{2}=1$.\n$q_{23}=\\frac{4-(-2)}{2}=3$.\nSo $p_{13}+q_{23}=1+3=4$.\nThe option $-2$ comes from wrongly using $q_{32}=-3$ in place of $q_{23}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":57,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A$, $B$, $C$ be matrices of the same order $2 \\times 3$, and let $k$, $l$ be scalars. How many of the following statements are always true?\n(i) $A - B = B - A$\n(ii) $(A + B) + C = A + (B + C)$\n(iii) $(k + l)A = kA + lA$\n(iv) $A + (-A) = O$, where $O$ is the $2 \\times 3$ zero matrix\n(v) $A + O$ is defined when $O$ is the $3 \\times 2$ zero matrix","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) False: $A - B = -(B - A)$, so the two are equal only when $A = B$.\n(ii) True: this is the associative law of addition.\n(iii) True: scalar multiplication distributes over the sum of scalars.\n(iv) True: $-A$ is the additive inverse, and the result is the zero matrix of the same order.\n(v) False: the orders differ, so the sum is not defined.\nSo 3 statements are true.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":58,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"For how many of the following matrix equations does at least one real value of x exist?\n(i) $\\begin{bmatrix} x & 2 \\\\ 3 & x \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$\n(ii) $\\begin{bmatrix} x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2x \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$\n(iii) $[x^2 \\;\\; 0] = [9 \\;\\; 0]$\n(iv) $\\begin{bmatrix} x \\\\ x \\end{bmatrix} = [2 \\;\\; 2]$\n(v) $[x^2 + 1] = [0]$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Possible: both diagonal entries give x = 4, which is consistent.\n(ii) Impossible: the (1,1) entry gives x = 3, but the (2,2) entry gives 2x = 5, so x = 2.5. These contradict each other.\n(iii) Possible: $x^2 = 9$ gives x = ±3.\n(iv) Impossible: the orders 2 × 1 and 1 × 2 differ.\n(v) Impossible: $x^2 = -1$ has no real solution.\nSo exactly 2 equations have a solution.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":59,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $3\\begin{bmatrix} x & 2 \\\\ 1 & y \\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 7 \\end{bmatrix}$, then the value of $x + y$ is","image":"","options":["7","5","9","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Scalar multiplication: the left side becomes $\\begin{bmatrix} 3x & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3y \\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 3x-4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 3y-5 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nCompare corresponding elements, using equality of matrices: $3x - 4 = 5 \\Rightarrow x = 3$, and $3y - 5 = 7 \\Rightarrow y = 4$.\nThe other entries, 5 = 5 and 3 = 3, are consistent.\nSo $x + y = 3 + 4 = 7$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":60,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let A and B be square matrices of the same order n (n ≥ 2). How many of the following statements are always true?\n(i) If A is both symmetric and skew symmetric, then A = O.\n(ii) If $A^2 = A$, then $(I + A)^3 = I + 7A$.\n(iii) $(A + B)^2 = A^2 + 2AB + B^2$.\n(iv) If A and B are symmetric, then AB is symmetric if and only if AB = BA.\n(v) Every diagonal element of $A - A'$ is zero.","image":"","options":["5","4","3","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) True: A' = A and A' = -A give A = -A, so A = O.\n(ii) True: $(I+A)^3 = I + 3A + 3A^2 + A^3$. With $A^2 = A$ we also get $A^3 = A$, so this equals I + 7A.\n(iii) False: $(A+B)^2 = A^2 + AB + BA + B^2$, and AB need not equal BA.\n(iv) True: $(AB)' = B'A' = BA$, so AB is symmetric exactly when BA = AB.\n(v) True: $A - A'$ is skew symmetric, so its diagonal elements are zero.\nSo 4 statements are true.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":61,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"For the square matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -6 & -3 \\end{bmatrix}$, what is the sum of the elements that form its diagonal?","image":"","options":["6","3","12","0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The diagonal of a square matrix consists of $a_{11}, a_{22}, a_{33}$.\nHere $a_{11} = 2$, $a_{22} = 7$ and $a_{33} = -3$.\nSum $= 2 + 7 - 3 = 6$.\nThe option 12 comes from adding the other diagonal, $5 + 7 + 4 + \\ldots$ style reading, which is not the diagonal defined in NCERT.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":62,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $\\begin{bmatrix} x+y & 2 \\\\ 5 & xy \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 12 \\end{bmatrix}$, then the value of $x^2 + y^2$ is:","image":"","options":["37","25","49","73"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Equating the corresponding entries gives x + y = 7 and xy = 12.\nUse the identity $x^2 + y^2 = (x+y)^2 - 2xy$.\nSubstituting: $x^2 + y^2 = 49 - 24 = 25$.\nCheck: (x, y) = (3, 4) or (4, 3), and 9 + 16 = 25.\nA common slip is adding 2xy instead of subtracting it, which gives 73.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":63,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 7 \\end{bmatrix}$. If X is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix such that $XA = B$, then the sum of all the elements of X is","image":"","options":["12","8","17","10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let X = [[a, b], [c, d]].\nThen XA = [[a, a + b], [c, c + d]].\nEquating with B: a = 3, a + b = 5 so b = 2, c = 2, c + d = 7 so d = 5.\nSo X = [[3, 2], [2, 5]], and the sum of its elements is 12.\nOption (2) is the trap: solving AX = B instead gives sum 8, because AX ≠ XA in general.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":64,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A = [a_{ij}]$ is defined by $a_{ij} = |2i - 3j|$. What is the sum of all the elements of $A$?","image":"","options":["20","18","22","16"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Row 1 ($i = 1$): $a_{11} = |2 - 3| = 1$, $a_{12} = |2 - 6| = 4$, $a_{13} = |2 - 9| = 7$.\nRow 2 ($i = 2$): $a_{21} = |4 - 3| = 1$, $a_{22} = |4 - 6| = 2$, $a_{23} = |4 - 9| = 5$.\nSo $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nSum $= 1 + 4 + 7 + 1 + 2 + 5 = 20$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":65,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A$ and $B$ be symmetric matrices of the same order.\nAssertion (A): $AB-BA$ is a skew-symmetric matrix.\nReason (R): For matrices of suitable orders, $(AB)'=B'A'$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: it is the reversal law for transposes.\nCheck A using R: $(AB-BA)'=(AB)'-(BA)'=B'A'-A'B'$.\nSince $A'=A$ and $B'=B$, this equals $BA-AB=-(AB-BA)$.\nSo $AB-BA$ is skew-symmetric. A is true, and the proof rests directly on R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":66,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A, B and C are matrices of orders $2 \\times 3$, $3 \\times 4$ and $4 \\times 2$ respectively. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) AB\n(b) BC\n(c) (AB)C\n(d) CA\nList-II\n(i) $2 \\times 2$\n(ii) $3 \\times 2$\n(iii) $4 \\times 3$\n(iv) $2 \\times 4$","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-i, b-ii, c-iv, d-iii","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Rule: (m × n)(n × p) gives an m × p matrix.\n(a) AB: (2×3)(3×4) gives 2×4, so (iv).\n(b) BC: (3×4)(4×2) gives 3×2, so (ii).\n(c) (AB)C: (2×4)(4×2) gives 2×2, so (i). This matches A(BC): (2×3)(3×2) = 2×2, as the associative law requires.\n(d) CA: (4×2)(2×3) gives 4×3, so (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":67,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Matrices X, Y and Z have orders 2 × 3, 3 × 4 and 4 × 2 respectively. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) XY\n(b) YZ\n(c) ZX\n(d) $(XYZ)'$\nList-II (Order)\n(i) 2 × 2\n(ii) 4 × 3\n(iii) 3 × 2\n(iv) 2 × 4","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"An (m × n) matrix times an (n × p) matrix gives an (m × p) matrix.\n(a) XY: (2 × 3)(3 × 4) gives 2 × 4, which is (iv).\n(b) YZ: (3 × 4)(4 × 2) gives 3 × 2, which is (iii).\n(c) ZX: (4 × 2)(2 × 3) gives 4 × 3, which is (ii).\n(d) XYZ: (2 × 4)(4 × 2) gives 2 × 2. Its transpose is also 2 × 2, which is (i).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":68,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A matrix A has 3 rows and 5 columns. What is the order of its transpose $A'$?","image":"","options":["$3 \\times 5$","$5 \\times 3$","$5 \\times 5$","$3 \\times 3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Taking the transpose turns the rows of A into columns and the columns into rows.\nIf $A = [a_{ij}]_{m \\times n}$, then $A' = [a_{ji}]_{n \\times m}$.\nHere m = 3 and n = 5, so $A'$ has 5 rows and 3 columns.\n$3 \\times 5$ is the order of A itself. The square orders are wrong because the number of elements must stay 15.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":69,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$, then the sum of all the elements of $A^5$ is","image":"","options":["112","144","128","160"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Write $A = 2I + N$ with $N = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$. Here $N^2 = O$, and $2I$ commutes with $N$.\nStep 2: By the binomial expansion, $A^n = 2^nI + n2^{n-1}N$. This can be confirmed by induction, as in NCERT Example 23.\nStep 3: For $n = 5$, $A^5 = \\begin{bmatrix} 32 & 80 \\\\ 0 & 32 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nStep 4: The sum of elements is $32 + 80 + 0 + 32 = 144$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":70,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, then the sum of all the elements of $A^3$ is","image":"","options":["8","10","7","12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind $A^2$: $A^2=A\\cdot A=\\begin{bmatrix}1&2+2\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1&4\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind $A^3$: $A^3=A^2A=\\begin{bmatrix}1&2+4\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1&6\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSum of the elements $=1+6+0+1=8$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":71,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$, then $(AB)^{-1}$ is","image":"","options":["$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Use the reversal law, $(AB)^{-1} = B^{-1}A^{-1}$.\nStep 2: Multiply $\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nRow 1 = [2−1, −3+2] = [1, −1].\nRow 2 = [0−1, 0+2] = [−1, 2].\nStep 3: So $(AB)^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nStep 4: Option (1) is $A^{-1}B^{-1}$, which comes from using the wrong order.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":72,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Every diagonal element of a skew-symmetric matrix is zero","For any square matrix $A$, the matrix $A+A'$ is symmetric","A matrix that is both symmetric and skew-symmetric must be the identity matrix","For any square matrix $A$, the matrix $A-A'$ is skew-symmetric"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: putting $i=j$ in $a_{ii}=-a_{ii}$ gives $a_{ii}=0$.\n(2) Correct: $(A+A')'=A'+A=A+A'$.\n(3) Incorrect: if $A'=A$ and $A'=-A$, then $A=-A$. So $2A=O$, which means $A=O$, the zero matrix, not $I$.\n(4) Correct: $(A-A')'=A'-A=-(A-A')$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":73,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A is a matrix of order $2 \\times 3$. Which of the following statements about the inverse of A is correct?","image":"","options":["A has an inverse of order $3 \\times 2$","A has no inverse, because only square matrices can be invertible","A has an inverse only if none of its elements is zero","A has an inverse of order $2 \\times 3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: B is the inverse of A only if AB = BA = I.\nStep 2: Equality needs AB and BA to have the same order. For the identity in both products to be the same matrix, A and B must be square matrices of the same order.\nStep 3: A is $2 \\times 3$, so it is rectangular. Hence A has no inverse.\nStep 4: Option (1) is the tempting one. If B were $3 \\times 2$, AB would be $2 \\times 2$ and BA would be $3 \\times 3$. These can never be equal.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":74,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A$ be a square matrix such that $A^2 = A$, and let $I$ be the identity matrix of the same order.\nStatement I: $(I - A)^2 = I - A$.\nStatement II: $(I + A)^2 = I + 3A$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Since $IA = AI = A$, the matrices $I$ and $A$ commute, so we can expand as in ordinary algebra.\nStatement I: $(I - A)^2 = I - 2A + A^2 = I - 2A + A = I - A$. True.\nStatement II: $(I + A)^2 = I + 2A + A^2 = I + 2A + A = I + 3A$. True.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":75,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): There are no real numbers x and y for which $\\begin{bmatrix} x & 0 \\\\ 0 & y \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nReason (R): Two matrices can be equal only if they are of the same order.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: the first condition for A = B is that A and B have the same order.\nA is true: the left matrix is 2 × 2, while the right matrix is 2 × 3.\nBecause the orders differ, no choice of x and y can make the matrices equal.\nSo R directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":76,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A$ and $B$ be matrices of order $m\\times n$ with $m\\neq n$, and let $k$, $l$ be scalars. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $A+B=B+A$\n(ii) $(k+l)A=kA+lA$\n(iii) If O is the zero matrix of order $n\\times m$, then $A+O=A$\n(iv) $A-B=B-A$\n(v) $k(A-B)=kA-kB$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: matrix addition is commutative because $a_{ij}+b_{ij}=b_{ij}+a_{ij}$ for every entry.\n(ii) Correct: $(k+l)a_{ij}=ka_{ij}+la_{ij}$ for every entry.\n(iii) Incorrect: the zero matrix of order $n\\times m$ does not have the same order as $A$ ($m\\times n$, with $m\\neq n$). So $A+O$ is not even defined. The additive identity must be the $m\\times n$ zero matrix.\n(iv) Incorrect: in general $A-B=-(B-A)$, so the two are not equal.\n(v) Correct: $k(A-B)=k(A+(-1)B)=kA+(-k)B=kA-kB$.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":77,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A matrix has only one column and contains 4 elements. What is its order?","image":"","options":["$1 \\times 4$","$4 \\times 1$","$2 \\times 2$","$4 \\times 4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A column matrix has exactly one column, so its order is $m \\times 1$.\nAn $m \\times 1$ matrix has $m$ elements, so $m = 4$.\nThe order is therefore $4 \\times 1$, read as rows × columns.\n$1 \\times 4$ is a row matrix, which is the order reversed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":78,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements.\nStatement I: There are no real numbers $a, b$ for which $\\begin{bmatrix} a & 2 \\\\ 3 & b \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} a & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & b & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nStatement II: If $\\begin{bmatrix} x+y & 2 \\\\ 5 & xy \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 6 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8 \\end{bmatrix}$, then the real numbers $x$ and $y$ are uniquely determined.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Two matrices can be equal only if they have the same order. Here the orders are 2 × 2 and 2 × 3. So equality is impossible for every $a, b$. True.\nStatement II: Equating entries gives $x+y=6$ and $xy=8$.\nSo $x$ and $y$ are the roots of $t^2-6t+8=0$, which are $t=2$ and $t=4$.\nThis gives two ordered pairs, $(x, y)=(2, 4)$ and $(4, 2)$. The values are not unique, so Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":79,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"For a matrix $A = [a_{ij}]$ of order $4 \\times 6$, which of the following elements does NOT exist?","image":"","options":["$a_{46}$","$a_{14}$","$a_{64}$","$a_{36}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In $a_{ij}$, $i$ is the row number and $j$ is the column number.\nFor a $4 \\times 6$ matrix, $1 \\le i \\le 4$ and $1 \\le j \\le 6$.\n$a_{46}$, $a_{14}$ and $a_{36}$ all have row ≤ 4 and column ≤ 6, so they exist.\n$a_{64}$ would sit in row 6, but the matrix has only 4 rows, so it does not exist.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":80,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A = [a_{ij}]$ is defined by $a_{ij} = 2i - 3j$ when $i \\ge j$, and $a_{ij} = i + j$ when $i < j$. What is the sum of all the elements of $A$?","image":"","options":["12","-2","10","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Case $i \\ge j$ uses $2i - 3j$: $a_{11} = -1$, $a_{21} = 1$, $a_{22} = -2$, $a_{31} = 3$, $a_{32} = 0$, $a_{33} = -3$. Their sum is $-2$.\nCase $i < j$ uses $i + j$: $a_{12} = 3$, $a_{13} = 4$, $a_{23} = 5$. Their sum is $12$.\nSo $A = \\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nTotal $= -2 + 12 = 10$. The tempting values 12 and $-2$ each count only one part of the matrix.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":81,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If A and B are both matrices of order $2 \\times 3$, then the product AB is not defined.\nReason (R): The product AB is defined only when the number of columns of A equals the number of rows of B.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. It is exactly the NCERT condition for AB to be defined.\nA is true. A has 3 columns but B has only 2 rows, so the condition fails.\nSo the truth of A follows directly from R. Hence R is the correct explanation of A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":82,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"The additive inverse of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & x \\end{bmatrix}$ is","image":"","options":["$\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -x \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -x \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -x \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{x} \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The additive inverse of $A$ is $-A = (-1)A$. It satisfies $A + (-A) = O$.\nMultiplying every element by $-1$ gives $\\begin{bmatrix} -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -x \\end{bmatrix}$.\nCheck: adding this to $A$ gives the zero matrix.\nOption (4) is wrong because it takes reciprocals of the elements. That has nothing to do with additive inverses.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":83,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): There exist non-zero square matrices $A$ and $B$ of order 2 such that $AB = O$.\nReason (R): Matrix multiplication is not commutative.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Take $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$. Then $AB = O$, although neither matrix is zero.\nR is true. In general $AB \\neq BA$; for instance $\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$ do not commute.\nR does not explain A. A shows that a product can vanish without either factor being zero, which is a separate property from commutativity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":84,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A$ be the $3\\times3$ matrix in which every element is 1. The sum of all the elements of $A+A^2+A^3+A^4$ is","image":"","options":["360","120","243","324"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Find $A^2$: each entry is a row times a column, $1+1+1=3$, so $A^2=3A$.\nHigher powers: $A^3=3A\\cdot A=3A^2=9A$, and $A^4=9A^2=27A$.\nAdd them: $A+A^2+A^3+A^4=(1+3+9+27)A=40A$.\nEach element of $40A$ is 40, and there are 9 elements, so the sum is $40\\times9=360$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":85,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A, B and C are non-zero square matrices of the same order. How many of the following statements are always true?\n(i) If AB = O, then BA = O.\n(ii) If AB = BA, then $(A + B)^2 = A^2 + 2AB + B^2$.\n(iii) If A = kI for a scalar k, then AB = BA.\n(iv) If AB = AC, then B = C.\n(v) A(B − C) = AB − AC","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) False. Take A = [[0, −1], [0, 2]] and B = [[3, 5], [0, 0]]. Then AB = O, but BA = [[0, 7], [0, 0]] ≠ O.\n(ii) True. (A + B)² = A² + AB + BA + B², and AB = BA gives A² + 2AB + B².\n(iii) True. (kI)B = kB = B(kI).\n(iv) False. With the A above, A·B = A·O = O, yet B ≠ O. So cancellation fails.\n(v) True, by the distributive law.\nSo 3 statements are true.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":86,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $\\begin{bmatrix} 2a & b+1 \\\\ c-3 & 4d \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 8 \\end{bmatrix}$, then $a + b + c + d$ equals:","image":"","options":["6","7","10","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Equate the corresponding entries of the two 2 × 2 matrices.\n2a = 6, so a = 3.\nb + 1 = 0, so b = −1.\nc − 3 = 1, so c = 4.\n4d = 8, so d = 2.\nSo a + b + c + d = 3 − 1 + 4 + 2 = 8.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":87,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $2\\begin{bmatrix} x & y \\\\ z & t \\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & y \\\\ t & 2 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6 \\end{bmatrix}$, then $x + y + z + t$ equals","image":"","options":["10","12","14","16"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"LHS $= \\begin{bmatrix} 2x-1 & y \\\\ 2z-t & 2t-2 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nEquate corresponding elements: $2x - 1 = 5$ gives $x = 3$, and $y = 3$.\n$2t - 2 = 6$ gives $t = 4$.\nThen $2z - t = 4$ gives $2z = 8$, so $z = 4$.\nSum $= 3 + 3 + 4 + 4 = 14$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":88,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If A is of order $2 \\times 3$ and B is of order $3 \\times 4$, then AB is defined but BA is not defined.\nStatement II: If both AB and BA are defined, then AB and BA must be of the same order.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: A has 3 columns and B has 3 rows, so AB is defined and is $2 \\times 4$. For BA, B has 4 columns but A has only 2 rows, so BA is not defined. Statement I is true.\nStatement II: Take A of order $2 \\times 3$ and B of order $3 \\times 2$. Both products are defined, but AB is $2 \\times 2$ while BA is $3 \\times 3$. Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":89,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Ravi needs 3 notebooks and 4 pens, while Sita needs 5 notebooks and 2 pens. Shop P charges ₹30 per notebook and ₹10 per pen. Shop Q charges ₹25 per notebook and ₹15 per pen. The requirement matrix $\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$ is multiplied by the price matrix $\\begin{bmatrix} 30 & 25 \\\\ 10 & 15 \\end{bmatrix}$. If each person buys everything from whichever shop is cheaper for them, what is the total amount the two spend together?","image":"","options":["₹285","₹300","₹290","₹265"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The product matrix has rows Ravi, Sita and columns P, Q.\nRow 1 (Ravi): $3(30)+4(10)=130$ at P and $3(25)+4(15)=135$ at Q.\nRow 2 (Sita): $5(30)+2(10)=170$ at P and $5(25)+2(15)=155$ at Q.\nSo Ravi buys from P for ₹130 and Sita buys from Q for ₹155.\nTotal = 130 + 155 = ₹285.\nIf both used P the total would be ₹300; if both used Q it would be ₹290.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":90,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The equation $\\begin{bmatrix} 2x & y \\\\ y & x \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$ has no solution in real numbers x, y.\nStatement II: If $\\begin{bmatrix} x & 1 \\\\ 1 & y \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} y & 1 \\\\ 1 & x \\end{bmatrix}$, then necessarily $x = y = 1$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: the (1,1) entry gives 2x = 6, so x = 3. The (2,2) entry gives x = 2. These contradict, so there is no solution. Statement I is true.\nStatement II: equating entries gives only x = y, and 1 = 1 holds automatically.\nAny equal pair works, for example x = y = 5, so x = y = 1 is not forced. Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":91,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following matrices is symmetric?","image":"","options":["$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 7 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A matrix is symmetric if $A' = A$, that is, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$.\n(1) $a_{12} = 2$ but $a_{21} = -2$, so it is not symmetric.\n(2) $a_{12} = a_{21} = 4$, so $A' = A$ and it is symmetric.\n(3) $a_{21} = -a_{12}$ and the diagonal is zero, so it is skew-symmetric, not symmetric.\n(4) $a_{12} = 3$ but $a_{21} = 1$, so it is not symmetric.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":92,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$ be written as A = P + Q, where P is symmetric and Q is skew symmetric.\nStatement I: $P = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$\nStatement II: The entry in the first row and second column of Q is –3.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The transpose is $A' = \\begin{bmatrix}3&2\\\\8&-1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nThe symmetric part is P = ½(A + A′) = ½$\\begin{bmatrix}6&10\\\\10&-2\\end{bmatrix}$ = $\\begin{bmatrix}3&5\\\\5&-1\\end{bmatrix}$. So Statement I is true.\nThe skew symmetric part is Q = ½(A – A′) = ½$\\begin{bmatrix}0&6\\\\-6&0\\end{bmatrix}$ = $\\begin{bmatrix}0&3\\\\-3&0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nThe (1, 2) entry of Q is 3, not –3, so Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":93,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $2\\begin{bmatrix} x & 1 \\\\ 3 & y \\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8 \\end{bmatrix}$, then $x + y$ equals","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"LHS $= \\begin{bmatrix} 2x+1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2y+2 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nEquate corresponding elements: $2x + 1 = 7$, so $x = 3$.\nAlso $2y + 2 = 8$, so $y = 3$.\nHence $x + y = 6$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":94,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A=\\begin{bmatrix} \\cos\\alpha & -\\sin\\alpha \\\\ \\sin\\alpha & \\cos\\alpha \\end{bmatrix}$, $0 < \\alpha < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, and $A + A' = \\sqrt{3}\\, I$, then $\\alpha$ equals:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{\\pi}{6}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{3}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$","$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The transpose is $A' = \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos\\alpha & \\sin\\alpha \\\\ -\\sin\\alpha & \\cos\\alpha \\end{bmatrix}$.\nAdding, $A + A' = \\begin{bmatrix} 2\\cos\\alpha & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\cos\\alpha \\end{bmatrix}$.\nEquating with $\\sqrt{3}I$ gives $2\\cos\\alpha = \\sqrt{3}$, so $\\cos\\alpha = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\nIn $(0, \\frac{\\pi}{2})$ this gives $\\alpha = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$. The value $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ would need $\\cos\\alpha = \\frac{1}{2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":95,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $X + Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 8 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $X - Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$, then $Y$ equals","image":"","options":["$\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Subtract the second equation from the first: $(X+Y) - (X-Y) = 2Y$.\nThis gives $2Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 4-2 & 6-0 \\\\ 2-(-4) & 8-2 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nSo $Y = \\frac{1}{2}(2Y) = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nOption (1) is $X$. Option (2) is $2Y$, where the student forgot to halve.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":96,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $\\begin{bmatrix} x+y & y+z & z+x \\\\ xyz & x^2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 7 & 6 \\\\ k & 4 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$, then the value of $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + k$ is:","image":"","options":["53","29","48","77"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The first row gives x + y = 5, y + z = 7 and z + x = 6.\nAdding these: 2(x + y + z) = 18, so x + y + z = 9.\nThen z = 9 − 5 = 4, x = 9 − 7 = 2 and y = 9 − 6 = 3.\nCheck the (2,2) entry: $x^2 = 4$ holds, so the system is consistent.\nThe (2,1) entry gives k = xyz = 24.\nSo $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + k = 4 + 9 + 16 + 24 = 53$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":97,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Every identity matrix is a scalar matrix.","Every scalar matrix is a diagonal matrix.","Every diagonal matrix is a scalar matrix.","The matrix $[5]$ of order $1 \\times 1$ is a diagonal as well as a scalar matrix."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: an identity matrix is a scalar matrix with $k = 1$.\n(2) Correct: a scalar matrix has all non-diagonal entries zero, so it is diagonal.\n(3) Incorrect: a diagonal matrix need not have equal diagonal entries. For example, $\\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$ is diagonal but not scalar.\n(4) Correct: $[5]$ has no non-diagonal entries, and its single diagonal entry is trivially constant.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":98,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a scalar matrix?","image":"","options":["$\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A scalar matrix is a diagonal matrix whose diagonal elements are all equal ($b_{ij}=0$ for $i \\ne j$ and $b_{ii}=k$).\n(1) It is diagonal, but the diagonal entries 3 and $-3$ are unequal, so it is not scalar.\n(2) Its off-diagonal entries are non-zero, so it is not even diagonal.\n(3) It is diagonal with both diagonal entries equal to 3, so it is scalar.\n(4) Its off-diagonal entries are non-zero, so it is not diagonal.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":99,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Every scalar matrix is an identity matrix.\nReason (R): An identity matrix is a scalar matrix in which the common diagonal element is 1.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. A scalar matrix has every diagonal element equal to some constant $k$. Only when $k = 1$ is it an identity matrix. For example, $\\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$ is scalar but is not $I$.\nR is true. $I_n$ is diagonal with all diagonal entries equal to 1, so it is a scalar matrix with $k = 1$.\nThe correct relation runs the other way: every identity matrix is scalar.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":100,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Shops P, Q and R sell tea and coffee. The daily sales are written as a $2 \\times 3$ matrix. The first row gives cups of tea and the second row gives cups of coffee. The columns correspond to shops P, Q and R in that order. What does the element $a_{23}$ represent?","image":"","options":["Cups of tea sold by shop R","Cups of coffee sold by shop Q","Cups of coffee sold by shop R","Cups of tea sold by shop Q"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$a_{23}$ lies in the 2nd row and the 3rd column.\nRow 2 is coffee, and column 3 is shop R.\nSo $a_{23}$ is the number of cups of coffee sold by shop R.\nThe option 'tea sold by shop R' reverses the roles: it would be $a_{13}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":101,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Matrices $A$, $B$, $C$ have orders $3 \\times 2$, $2 \\times 4$ and $4 \\times 3$ respectively. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(P) $AB$\n(Q) $BC$\n(R) $CA$\n(S) $ABC$\nList-II:\n(1) $4 \\times 2$\n(2) $3 \\times 3$\n(3) $3 \\times 4$\n(4) $2 \\times 3$","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2","P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2","P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3","P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Rule: if the first matrix is $m \\times n$ and the second is $n \\times p$, the product is $m \\times p$.\nP: $(3 \\times 2)(2 \\times 4) = 3 \\times 4$, so P-3.\nQ: $(2 \\times 4)(4 \\times 3) = 2 \\times 3$, so Q-4.\nR: $(4 \\times 3)(3 \\times 2) = 4 \\times 2$, so R-1.\nS: $(AB)C = (3 \\times 4)(4 \\times 3) = 3 \\times 3$, so S-2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":102,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For any matrix A of order $m \\times n$, both $AA'$ and $A'A$ are symmetric matrices.\nReason (R): $(PQ)' = Q'P'$ and $(P')' = P$ for matrices of suitable orders.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Orders: $AA'$ is $m \\times m$ and $A'A$ is $n \\times n$, so both are square.\nFor the first: $(AA')' = (A')'A' = AA'$, so $AA'$ is symmetric.\nFor the second: $(A'A)' = A'(A')' = A'A$, so $A'A$ is symmetric.\nR states standard transpose properties, so R is true. These are exactly the properties used to prove A.\nHence both are true and R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":103,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let A be a matrix of order $3 \\times 4$ whose element $a_{23} = 7$. Which of the following is correct about the transpose $A'$?","image":"","options":["$A'$ is of order $4 \\times 3$ and its (3, 2)th element is 7","$A'$ is of order $3 \\times 4$ and its (2, 3)th element is 7","$A'$ is of order $4 \\times 3$ and its (2, 3)th element is 7","$A'$ is of order $3 \\times 4$ and its (3, 2)th element is 7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Definition: if $A = [a_{ij}]_{m \\times n}$, then $A' = [a_{ji}]_{n \\times m}$, so rows and columns are interchanged.\nOrder: A is $3 \\times 4$, so $A'$ is $4 \\times 3$. This rules out options (2) and (4).\nPosition: the element in row 2, column 3 of A moves to row 3, column 2 of $A'$. So the (3, 2)th element of $A'$ is 7.\nOption (3) gets the order right but forgets that the indices also swap.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":104,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about types of matrices is correct?","image":"","options":["Every diagonal matrix is a scalar matrix.","Every identity matrix is a scalar matrix.","Every scalar matrix is an identity matrix.","A zero matrix must always be a square matrix."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: A diagonal matrix only needs its non-diagonal elements to be zero. Its diagonal elements may differ, as in $\\begin{bmatrix}-1&0\\\\0&2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n(2) Correct: An identity matrix is diagonal and all its diagonal elements equal 1. So it is a scalar matrix with $k = 1$.\n(3) Incorrect: A scalar matrix is an identity matrix only when $k = 1$. For example, $\\begin{bmatrix}3&0\\\\0&3\\end{bmatrix}$ is scalar but not the identity.\n(4) Incorrect: $[0\\;\\;0]$ is a $1\\times 2$ zero matrix, and it is not square.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":105,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Matrices X and Y satisfy $2X + 3Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $3X + 2Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nStatement I: $X - Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$\nStatement II: $Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Subtract the first equation from the second: $X - Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$. So Statement I is correct.\nAdd the two equations: $5(X+Y) = \\begin{bmatrix} 10 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$, so $X + Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nThen $2Y = (X+Y) - (X-Y) = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$, giving $Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nThe (1,2) entry of Y is $-1$, not 1, so Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":106,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A$ and $B$ be symmetric matrices and $C$ a skew-symmetric matrix, all square and of the same order. How many of the following matrices are skew-symmetric?\n(i) $AB-BA$\n(ii) $C^2$\n(iii) $AC+CA$\n(iv) $AC-CA$\n(v) $C^3$\n(vi) $BCB$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Use $A'=A$, $B'=B$, $C'=-C$ and $(XY)'=Y'X'$.\n(i) $(AB-BA)'=BA-AB$, so it is skew-symmetric.\n(ii) $(C^2)'=(-C)(-C)=C^2$, so it is symmetric.\n(iii) $(AC+CA)'=-CA-AC$, so it is skew-symmetric.\n(iv) $(AC-CA)'=-CA+AC=AC-CA$, so it is symmetric.\n(v) $(C^3)'=(-C)^3=-C^3$, so it is skew-symmetric.\n(vi) $(BCB)'=B(-C)B=-BCB$, so it is skew-symmetric.\nThe skew-symmetric ones are (i), (iii), (v) and (vi), giving a count of 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":107,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A$ be the diagonal matrix of order 3 with diagonal elements $2, -1, 4$ (in that order). If $B$ is a diagonal matrix of order 3 such that $AB = I_3$, then what is the sum of the diagonal elements of $B$?","image":"","options":["1.75","5","0.25","-0.25"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Let $B = \\text{diag}(p, q, r)$.\nThe product of two diagonal matrices of the same order is diagonal, with entries multiplied position by position: $AB = \\text{diag}(2p, -q, 4r)$.\nSetting $AB = I_3$ gives $2p = 1$, $-q = 1$ and $4r = 1$.\nSo $p = \\frac{1}{2}$, $q = -1$ and $r = \\frac{1}{4}$.\nSum $= 0.5 - 1 + 0.25 = -0.25$. The option 1.75 comes from wrongly taking $q = 1$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":108,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"For square matrices A, B and C of the same order, which of the following statements is always true?","image":"","options":["AB = BA","If AB = O, then A = O or B = O","(AB)C = A(BC)","If AB = AC, then B = C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) False: matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\n(2) False: two non-zero matrices can have a zero product (NCERT Example 15).\n(3) True: the associative law (AB)C = A(BC) holds whenever both sides are defined, and they are defined here.\n(4) False: cancellation fails. For example, if A = O then AB = AC for any B and C.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":109,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let A be a square matrix such that $A^2 = A$, and let I be the identity matrix of the same order. How many of the following statements are true for every such matrix A?\n(i) $(I - A)^2 = I - A$\n(ii) $(I + A)^2 = I + 2A$\n(iii) $(I - 2A)^2 = I$\n(iv) $A^3 = A$\n(v) $A(I - A) = I$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) True: $(I-A)^2 = I - 2A + A^2 = I - 2A + A = I - A$.\n(ii) False: $(I+A)^2 = I + 2A + A^2 = I + 3A$. For A = I, this gives 4I, not 3I.\n(iii) True: $(I-2A)^2 = I - 4A + 4A^2 = I - 4A + 4A = I$.\n(iv) True: $A^3 = A^2 \\cdot A = A \\cdot A = A^2 = A$.\n(v) False: $A(I - A) = A - A^2 = O$, which is not I.\nSo 3 statements are true.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":110,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $2\\begin{bmatrix} x & 3 \\\\ 1 & y \\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -2 \\\\ y & 4 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 7 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 10 \\end{bmatrix}$, then the value of $xy$ is","image":"","options":["6","12","9","$\\frac{15}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The left side is $\\begin{bmatrix} 2x + 1 & 6 - 2 \\\\ 2 + y & 2y + 4 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nEquate corresponding elements:\n$2x + 1 = 7$ gives $x = 3$.\n$2 + y = 5$ gives $y = 3$.\nCheck: $2y + 4 = 10$ ✓ and $6 - 2 = 4$ ✓, so the system is consistent.\nSo $xy = 3 \\times 3 = 9$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":111,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"The vertices of triangle PQR are P(2, –1), Q(0, 3) and R(4, 5). The triangle is written as a matrix X. Each vertex forms one column, taken in the order P, Q, R. The first row holds the x-coordinates and the second row holds the y-coordinates. What are the order of X and the value of its element $a_{21}$?","image":"","options":["Order $2 \\times 3$, $a_{21} = -1$","Order $3 \\times 2$, $a_{21} = 0$","Order $2 \\times 3$, $a_{21} = 0$","Order $3 \\times 2$, $a_{21} = -1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"X has 2 rows (x and y) and 3 columns (P, Q, R), so its order is $2 \\times 3$.\n$X = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$\n$a_{21}$ is the element in row 2, column 1. This is the y-coordinate of P, which is $-1$.\nThe value 0 is $a_{12}$ (the x-coordinate of Q). Choosing it means the row and column indices were swapped.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":112,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$. The element in the second row and first column of $3A - 2B$ is","image":"","options":["5","17","1","13"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $a_{21} = 3$ and $b_{21} = -2$.\nFormula: the $(i,j)$th element of $3A - 2B$ is $3a_{ij} - 2b_{ij}$.\nSubstituting: $3(3) - 2(-2) = 9 + 4$.\nSo the element is 13.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":113,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1&2\\\\2&4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix}2&x\\\\-1&-1\\end{bmatrix}$ are such that $AB = O$, then the value of $x$ is","image":"","options":["2","-2","1","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Multiply row by column:\n$AB = \\begin{bmatrix}1(2)+2(-1) & 1(x)+2(-1)\\\\ 2(2)+4(-1) & 2(x)+4(-1)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & x-2\\\\ 0 & 2x-4\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFor $AB = O$, every entry must be zero, so $x - 2 = 0$ and $2x - 4 = 0$.\nBoth conditions give $x = 2$.\nNote that A and B are both non-zero, yet their product is O.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":114,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For any two matrices $A$ and $B$ of the same order, $A - B = -(B - A)$.\nReason (R): $-(B - A) = (-1)(B + (-1)A) = (-1)B + A$, using $k(A+B) = kA + kB$, and matrix addition is commutative.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. By definition $B - A = B + (-1)A$.\nApplying $k(X+Y) = kX + kY$ with $k = -1$ gives $(-1)B + (-1)(-1)A = -B + A$.\nBy the commutative law, $-B + A = A + (-B) = A - B$. So A is true.\nThe reasoning in R is exactly what proves A, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":115,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Matrix A has order $3 \\times 4$ and matrix B has order $4 \\times 2$. What is the order of the product AB?","image":"","options":["$3 \\times 2$","$4 \\times 4$","$2 \\times 3$","$3 \\times 4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"AB is defined only when the number of columns of A equals the number of rows of B. Here both are 4, so AB is defined.\nIf A is $m \\times n$ and B is $n \\times p$, then AB is $m \\times p$.\nWith m = 3 and p = 2, AB is $3 \\times 2$.\n$4 \\times 4$ wrongly uses the matching inner dimension. $2 \\times 3$ reverses the order.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":116,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = [a_{ij}]$ and $B = [b_{ij}]$ be $2 \\times 3$ matrices with $a_{ij} = i - j$ and $b_{ij} = i + j$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(P) $(1,3)$th element of $A + B$\n(Q) $(2,3)$th element of $A - B$\n(R) $(2,1)$th element of $3A - B$\n(S) $(1,2)$th element of $(2 + 3)A$\nList-II\n(1) 0\n(2) $-6$\n(3) 2\n(4) $-5$","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4","P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4","P-2, Q-3, R-1, S-4","P-3, Q-2, R-4, S-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Work out the general element of each matrix first.\n(P) $a_{ij} + b_{ij} = 2i$. For $i = 1$ this is 2, so P-3.\n(Q) $a_{ij} - b_{ij} = -2j$. For $j = 3$ this is $-6$, so Q-2.\n(R) $3(i - j) - (i + j) = 2i - 4j$. For $(2,1)$ this is $4 - 4 = 0$, so R-1.\n(S) $(2+3)A = 5A$, with element $5(i - j)$. For $(1,2)$ this is $5(1 - 2) = -5$, so S-4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":117,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let A, B and C be matrices of orders $2 \\times 3$, $3 \\times 2$ and $2 \\times 2$ respectively. How many of the following are defined?\nAB,  BA + C,  A + B',  AC,  BC,  CA","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"AB: (2 × 3)(3 × 2) gives a 2 × 2 matrix, so it is defined.\nBA + C: BA is 3 × 3 and C is 2 × 2. They cannot be added, so it is not defined.\nA + B': B' is 2 × 3, the same order as A, so it is defined.\nAC: A has 3 columns but C has 2 rows, so it is not defined.\nBC: (3 × 2)(2 × 2) gives a 3 × 2 matrix, so it is defined.\nCA: (2 × 2)(2 × 3) gives a 2 × 3 matrix, so it is defined.\nSo 4 of them are defined.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":118,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Matrices P, Q, R and S have orders $p \\times 3$, $3 \\times 4$, $2 \\times r$ and $r \\times 4$ respectively. The restriction on p and r so that PQ + RS is defined is","image":"","options":["p = 2, r is arbitrary","p = 2 and r = 2","r = 3, p is arbitrary","p = 3 and r = 4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"PQ is (p×3)(3×4). It is always defined and has order p×4.\nRS is (2×r)(r×4). It is always defined, for any r, and has order 2×4.\nA sum is defined only for matrices of the same order, so p×4 must equal 2×4, giving p = 2.\nThere is no condition on r, so r is arbitrary. The sum is then 2×4.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":119,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let A be a matrix of order 2 × 3 and B be a matrix of order 3 × 4.\nStatement I: AB is defined and is of order 2 × 4.\nStatement II: BA is also defined.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A product PQ is defined only when the number of columns of P equals the number of rows of Q.\nStatement I is true: for AB, A has 3 columns and B has 3 rows. The product has order (rows of A) × (columns of B) = 2 × 4.\nStatement II is false: for BA, B has 4 columns but A has only 2 rows, and 4 ≠ 2.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":120,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"The matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$ is written as $A = P + Q$, where $P = [p_{ij}]$ is symmetric and $Q = [q_{ij}]$ is skew symmetric. The value of $p_{23} + q_{13}$ is","image":"","options":["-2","2","-6","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: $P = \\frac{1}{2}(A + A')$ and $Q = \\frac{1}{2}(A - A')$.\nSo $p_{ij} = \\frac{a_{ij} + a_{ji}}{2}$ and $q_{ij} = \\frac{a_{ij} - a_{ji}}{2}$.\nSubstituting: $p_{23} = \\frac{5 + (-1)}{2} = 2$.\nAlso $q_{13} = \\frac{-2 - 6}{2} = -4$.\nSo $p_{23} + q_{13} = 2 - 4 = -2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":121,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos\\theta & \\sin\\theta \\\\ -\\sin\\theta & \\cos\\theta \\end{bmatrix}$ with $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{9}$. The smallest positive integer $n$ for which $A^n = I$ is","image":"","options":["9","18","36","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"By induction (NCERT Example 23), $A^n = \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos n\\theta & \\sin n\\theta \\\\ -\\sin n\\theta & \\cos n\\theta \\end{bmatrix}$.\nFor $A^n = I$ we need $\\cos n\\theta = 1$ and $\\sin n\\theta = 0$. So $n\\theta$ must be a multiple of $2\\pi$.\nWith $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{9}$: $\\frac{n\\pi}{9} = 2\\pi$, so the smallest such $n$ is 18.\n$n = 9$ fails because it gives $\\cos\\pi = -1$, so $A^9 = -I$, not $I$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":122,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If A is a column matrix of order $3 \\times 1$ and B is a row matrix of order $1 \\times 3$, then AB is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix while BA is a $1 \\times 1$ matrix.\nReason (R): The product of an $m \\times n$ matrix and an $n \\times p$ matrix is a matrix of order $m \\times p$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is the NCERT rule for the order of a product, so R is true.\nAB is (3×1)(1×3). Here n = 1, so AB has order 3×3.\nBA is (1×3)(3×1). Here n = 3, so BA has order 1×1.\nSo A is true, and it follows directly from R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":123,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let A, B and C be invertible square matrices of the same order.\nStatement I: $(ABC)^{-1} = C^{-1}B^{-1}A^{-1}$.\nStatement II: The matrix $A + B$ is always invertible.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: Apply the reversal law twice:\n$(ABC)^{-1} = ((AB)C)^{-1} = C^{-1}(AB)^{-1} = C^{-1}B^{-1}A^{-1}$.\nSo Statement I is true.\nStep 2: Test Statement II with B = −A. B is invertible, with inverse $-A^{-1}$.\nStep 3: Then A + B = O. The zero matrix has no inverse, since OX = O ≠ I for every X.\nSo Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":124,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A matrix has exactly 12 elements. How many different orders are possible for it?","image":"","options":["4","6","8","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: an $m \\times n$ matrix has $mn$ elements, so we need $mn = 12$ with $m, n \\in N$.\nOrdered pairs: (1,12), (12,1), (2,6), (6,2), (3,4), (4,3).\nOrder matters, because $3 \\times 4$ and $4 \\times 3$ are different orders.\nSo there are 6 possible orders.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":125,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about equality of matrices is correct?","image":"","options":["A $1 \\times 3$ zero matrix and a $3 \\times 1$ zero matrix are equal, since all their entries are zero.","If A = B, then A and B have the same order and hence the same number of elements.","Two matrices containing the same set of numbers are equal, whatever the positions of those numbers.","The equation $[a \\;\\; b] = [b \\;\\; a]$ gives no information about a and b."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: the orders 1 × 3 and 3 × 1 differ, so the matrices cannot be equal.\n(2) Correct: equality requires the same order m × n, so both matrices have mn elements.\n(3) Incorrect: each entry must match in position. For example, [[2,3],[0,1]] ≠ [[3,2],[0,1]].\n(4) Incorrect: comparing the (1,1) entries gives a = b.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":126,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & p \\\\ -2 & -3 \\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $A^2 = I$, then $p$ equals","image":"","options":["4","$-4$","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Compute $A^2$:\n(1,1) entry: $9 - 2p$. (1,2) entry: $3p - 3p = 0$. (2,1) entry: $-6 + 6 = 0$. (2,2) entry: $-2p + 9$.\nSo $A^2 = (9 - 2p)I$.\nSetting $A^2 = I$ gives $9 - 2p = 1$, so $p = 4$.\nThe value $-4$ would give $9 + 8 = 17 \\neq 1$, so it fails.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":127,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$, then which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["$AB = BA = \\begin{bmatrix} 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 \\end{bmatrix}$","$AB = \\begin{bmatrix} 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 \\end{bmatrix}$ but $BA = \\begin{bmatrix} 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$","$AB = BA = \\begin{bmatrix} 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$","$AB = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"When two diagonal matrices of the same order are multiplied, the result is diagonal. Each diagonal entry is the product of the corresponding diagonal entries.\nAB = diag(2·5, 3·(−1)) = diag(10, −3).\nBA = diag(5·2, (−1)·3) = diag(10, −3).\nSo AB = BA, since diagonal matrices of the same order commute.\nOption (3) adds the entries instead of multiplying them.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":128,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nAssertion (A): $AB \\neq BA$.\nReason (R): For any two square matrices A and B of the same order, $AB \\neq BA$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Compute AB = [[1+2, 0+2], [0+1, 0+1]] = [[3, 2], [1, 1]].\nCompute BA = [[1, 2], [1, 2+1]] = [[1, 2], [1, 3]].\nSince AB ≠ BA, A is true.\nR is false: some pairs do commute. For example, diagonal matrices of the same order commute, and so do A and I.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":129,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A' = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$, then $(A + 2B)'$ equals","image":"","options":["$\\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 5 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Use the properties: $(A + 2B)' = A' + (2B)' = A' + 2B'$.\nFirst, $B' = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$, so $2B' = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nThen $A' + 2B' = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nOption (1) is $A' + 2B$, which forgets to transpose B. Option (2) is $A + 2B$ itself. Option (4) is $A' + B'$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":130,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements.\nStatement I: Every scalar matrix is an identity matrix.\nStatement II: Every identity matrix is a scalar matrix.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A scalar matrix is a diagonal matrix with every diagonal element equal to some constant k.\nStatement I is false: for example $\\begin{bmatrix}3&0\\\\0&3\\end{bmatrix}$ is scalar with k = 3, but it is not $I_2$.\nStatement II is true: an identity matrix is the scalar matrix with k = 1.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":131,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"The product $\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} 4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\end{bmatrix}$ equals the $1 \\times 1$ matrix","image":"","options":["[3]","[-3]","[9]","[-15]"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A $1 \\times 3$ matrix times a $3 \\times 1$ matrix gives a $1 \\times 1$ matrix.\nMultiply the entries element-wise and add: $2(4) + (-1)(5) + 3(-2)$.\nThis is $8 - 5 - 6 = -3$.\nSo the product is [-3].\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":132,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $A^2 - 2A = kI$, where $I$ is the identity matrix of order 2, then $k$ equals:","image":"","options":["$-3$","$3$","$-1$","$2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Compute $A^2$: $A^2 = \\begin{bmatrix} 4-3 & -2+0 \\\\ 6+0 & -3+0 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nCompute $2A$: $2A = \\begin{bmatrix} 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nSubtract: $A^2 - 2A = \\begin{bmatrix} -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 \\end{bmatrix} = -3I$.\nSo $k = -3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":133,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A$ be a skew-symmetric matrix and $B$ a symmetric matrix of the same order. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["$A^2$ is symmetric","$A^3$ is skew-symmetric","$AB - BA$ is symmetric","$AB + BA$ is symmetric"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"We have $A' = -A$ and $B' = B$.\n(1) Correct: $(A^2)' = (A')^2 = (-A)^2 = A^2$.\n(2) Correct: $(A^3)' = (-A)^3 = -A^3$.\n(3) Correct: $(AB - BA)' = B'A' - A'B' = -BA + AB = AB - BA$, so it is symmetric.\n(4) Incorrect: $(AB + BA)' = B'A' + A'B' = -BA - AB = -(AB + BA)$, so it is skew-symmetric, not symmetric.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":134,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let A and B be symmetric matrices of the same order.\nAssertion (A): AB – BA is a skew symmetric matrix.\nReason (R): Matrix multiplication is associative, that is, (AB)C = A(BC) whenever both sides are defined.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Check A: (AB – BA)′ = (AB)′ – (BA)′ = B′A′ – A′B′.\nSince A′ = A and B′ = B, this equals BA – AB = –(AB – BA). So A is true.\nR is also true, because associativity is a basic property of matrix multiplication.\nHowever, the proof of A uses the reversal rule (AB)′ = B′A′ and the symmetry of A and B, not associativity. So R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":135,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A(\\alpha) = \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos\\alpha & \\sin\\alpha \\\\ -\\sin\\alpha & \\cos\\alpha \\end{bmatrix}$.\nStatement I: $[A(\\alpha)]' = A(-\\alpha)$.\nStatement II: $[A(\\alpha)]'\\,A(\\alpha) = I$ for every real $\\alpha$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: $A' = \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos\\alpha & -\\sin\\alpha \\\\ \\sin\\alpha & \\cos\\alpha \\end{bmatrix}$. Since $\\cos(-\\alpha) = \\cos\\alpha$ and $\\sin(-\\alpha) = -\\sin\\alpha$, this is exactly $A(-\\alpha)$. True.\nStatement II: In $A'A$, the (1,1) entry is $\\cos^2\\alpha + \\sin^2\\alpha = 1$. The (1,2) entry is $\\cos\\alpha\\sin\\alpha - \\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha = 0$. The rest follow similarly, so $A'A = I$. True.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":136,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"P, Q, R and T are matrices of orders $2 \\times 3$, $3 \\times n$, $2 \\times k$ and $k \\times 4$ respectively. The expression PQ + RT is defined if and only if","image":"","options":["n = 4, k arbitrary","n = 3, k = 4","n = 4, k = 2","n = 2, k = 3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"PQ is always defined, since 3 = 3. Its order is $2 \\times n$.\nRT is always defined, since k = k. Its order is $2 \\times 4$, whatever k is.\nTwo matrices can be added only if they have the same order. So we need $2 \\times n = 2 \\times 4$, which gives n = 4.\nNo condition is placed on k, so k is arbitrary.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":137,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Matrices A, B, C and D have orders $2 \\times 3$, $3 \\times 3$, $3 \\times 1$ and $1 \\times 2$ respectively. How many of the following products are defined?\nAB, BA, AC, CA, DA, CD","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Check whether columns of the first matrix equal rows of the second.\nAB: (2×3)(3×3), 3 = 3, defined.\nBA: (3×3)(2×3), 3 ≠ 2, not defined.\nAC: (2×3)(3×1), defined.\nCA: (3×1)(2×3), 1 ≠ 2, not defined.\nDA: (1×2)(2×3), defined.\nCD: (3×1)(1×2), defined.\nSo 4 of the products are defined.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":138,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct? (All matrices have real entries, and each equation involves matrices of the same order.)\n(i) If $A$ and $B$ are $3 \\times 2$ matrices and $2A + 3X = 5B$, then $X$ must be a $3 \\times 2$ matrix.\n(ii) If $X + Y = P$ and $X - Y = Q$, then $Y = \\frac{1}{2}(Q - P)$.\n(iii) If $kA = O$ for some non-zero scalar $k$, then $A = O$.\n(iv) If $A + X = A + Y$, then $X = Y$.\n(v) If $2X + 3Y = P$ and $4X + 6Y = Q$, then $X$ and $Y$ can always be determined uniquely.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: addition is defined only for matrices of the same order, so $3X$, and hence X, must be $3 \\times 2$.\n(ii) Incorrect: subtracting the equations gives $2Y = P - Q$, so $Y = \\frac{1}{2}(P - Q)$.\n(iii) Correct: each $k a_{ij} = 0$ with $k \\neq 0$ forces $a_{ij} = 0$.\n(iv) Correct: adding $-A$ to both sides leaves $X = Y$.\n(v) Incorrect: the second equation is just twice the first in X and Y, so it gives no new information.\nCorrect statements: 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":139,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A is a matrix of order $2 \\times 3$. If $I_m A = A$ and $A I_n = A$, where $I_m$ and $I_n$ are identity matrices, then","image":"","options":["m = 3, n = 2","m = n = 2","m = n = 3","m = 2, n = 3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"For $I_m A$ to be defined, the number of columns of $I_m$ must equal the number of rows of A, which is 2. So m = 2.\nFor $A I_n$ to be defined, the number of columns of A (which is 3) must equal the number of rows of $I_n$. So n = 3.\nBoth products then have order 2 × 3 and equal A.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":140,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Two friends buy notebooks and pens. Their requirements are given by the matrix $R = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$ (row 1: Asha, row 2: Kabir; column 1: notebooks, column 2: pens). The prices are ₹30 per notebook and ₹10 per pen, written as $P = \\begin{bmatrix} 30 \\\\ 10 \\end{bmatrix}$. Using RP, the amount Kabir has to spend is","image":"","options":["₹130","₹170","₹300","₹110"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"RP is a $2 \\times 1$ matrix. Its second entry is the money Kabir needs.\nKabir's row [5 2] times the price column gives $5 \\times 30 + 2 \\times 10$.\nThis is $150 + 20 = 170$.\n₹130 is Asha's amount, and ₹300 is the total for both friends.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":141,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"How many different orders are possible for a matrix that has exactly 30 elements?","image":"","options":["4","6","8","16"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"An $m \\times n$ matrix has $mn$ elements, so we need ordered pairs of natural numbers $(m, n)$ with $mn = 30$.\nThe divisors of 30 are 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 10, 15 and 30. Each divisor $m$ fixes $n = \\frac{30}{m}$.\nThe orders are 1×30, 30×1, 2×15, 15×2, 3×10, 10×3, 5×6 and 6×5.\nThat makes 8 orders. Counting only unordered pairs gives 4, which is wrong because $2 \\times 15$ and $15 \\times 2$ are different orders.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":142,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$. The product AB is","image":"","options":["$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Row 1 of A times column 1 of B: 1(1) + 1(−1) = 0.\nRow 1 of A times column 2 of B: 1(−1) + 1(1) = 0.\nRow 2 of A equals row 1, so it gives the same two zeros.\nSo AB = O even though A ≠ O and B ≠ O. Unlike real numbers, a zero product does not force either factor to be zero.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":143,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A = [a_{ij}]$ has elements given by $a_{ij} = i^2 - j$. The sum of the diagonal elements of A is","image":"","options":["8","6","14","12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: the diagonal elements are $a_{11}, a_{22}, a_{33}$. Each has the form $i^2 - i$.\nSubstituting: $a_{11} = 1 - 1 = 0$, $a_{22} = 4 - 2 = 2$, $a_{33} = 9 - 3 = 6$.\nSo the sum is $0 + 2 + 6 = 8$.\nOption 14 comes from wrongly adding $i^2$ for all three, giving $1 + 4 + 9 = 14$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":144,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1\\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4\\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$. What is the sum of all the elements of the matrix $3A-2B$?","image":"","options":["$-4$","$28$","$4$","$-10$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A$ and $B$ are both of order $2\\times 2$, so $3A-2B$ is defined.\nScalar multiplication: $3A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3\\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$ and $2B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8\\\\ -4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$.\nDifference: $3A-2B=3A+(-2B)=\\begin{bmatrix}6-2 & -3-8\\\\ 0+4 & 9-10\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -11\\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSum of the elements: $4-11+4-1=-4$.\nThe distractor 28 is the sum for $3A+2B$, which comes from adding instead of subtracting.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":145,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$, $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $2A + 3X = 5B$, then the sum of all the elements of $X$ is","image":"","options":["11","13","15","39"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rearranging gives $3X = 5B - 2A$.\n$5B - 2A = \\begin{bmatrix} 10-4 & 5+4 \\\\ 15-6 & 15-0 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 6 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 15 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nSo $X = \\frac{1}{3}(5B-2A) = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nSum of elements $= 2 + 3 + 3 + 5 = 13$.\nThe option 39 comes from forgetting to divide by 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":146,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If $X + Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 6 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $X - Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$, then $X = \\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nReason (R): Adding the two equations gives $(X+Y)+(X-Y) = 2X$, because matrix addition is commutative and associative and $Y + (-Y) = O$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: $(X+Y)+(X-Y) = (X+X)+(Y-Y) = 2X + O = 2X$. This step uses the commutative and associative laws and the additive inverse.\nA: $2X = \\begin{bmatrix} 8 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 12 \\end{bmatrix}$, so $X = \\begin{bmatrix} 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 \\end{bmatrix}$. A is true.\nR is exactly the method that gives A, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":147,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Every entry of a $3\\times 3$ matrix is either 0 or 1. How many such matrices are diagonal matrices but NOT scalar matrices?","image":"","options":["8","6","2","512"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: In a diagonal matrix all 6 non-diagonal entries must be 0. Each of the 3 diagonal entries can be 0 or 1. This gives $2^3 = 8$ diagonal matrices.\nStep 2: A scalar matrix has all diagonal entries equal. With entries 0 or 1, the diagonal is either all 0 or all 1. That gives only O ($k=0$) and $I_3$ ($k=1$), so there are 2 scalar matrices.\nStep 3: Diagonal but not scalar $= 8 - 2 = 6$.\nThe option 8 forgets to remove O and I. The option 512 $= 2^9$ counts all 0-1 matrices of order 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":148,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"The matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & a & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & b \\\\ c & -4 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$ is skew symmetric. The value of $a + b + c$ is","image":"","options":["-1","1","5","-9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A is skew symmetric, so $a_{ji} = -a_{ij}$ for all $i$ and $j$.\nFrom $a_{12} = -a_{21}$: $a = -2$.\nFrom $a_{23} = -a_{32}$: $b = -(-4) = 4$.\nFrom $a_{31} = -a_{13}$: $c = -3$.\nSo $a + b + c = -2 + 4 - 3 = -1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":149,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For the $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A = [a_{ij}]$ with $a_{ij} = i - j$, the element in the third row and first column is 2.\nReason (R): $a_{ij}$ denotes the element lying in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the matrix.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: this is exactly how NCERT defines the $(i, j)$th element.\nUsing R, the element in row 3 and column 1 is $a_{31}$.\n$a_{31} = 3 - 1 = 2$, so A is true.\nA follows directly from R, so R is the correct explanation of A. A common slip is to evaluate $a_{13} = -2$ instead.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":150,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $\\begin{bmatrix} x+2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & y-1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6 \\end{bmatrix}$, then the value of $x + y$ is:","image":"","options":["8","12","10","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Both matrices are 2 × 2, so we equate corresponding entries.\n(1,1) entry: x + 2 = 5, so x = 3.\n(2,2) entry: y − 1 = 6, so y = 7.\nThe (1,2) and (2,1) entries (3 = 3 and 4 = 4) are already consistent.\nSo x + y = 3 + 7 = 10.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":151,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = [1\\ \\ 4]$, then $(AB)'$ is","image":"","options":["$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 12 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 12 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 12 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 8 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 12 \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is $3 \\times 1$ and B is $1 \\times 2$, so AB is $3 \\times 2$: $AB = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 12 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nTransposing gives a $2 \\times 3$ matrix: $\\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 12 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nCheck with $B'A'$: $\\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{bmatrix}[2\\ -1\\ 3]$ gives the same matrix.\nOption (1) is AB itself, and option (3) has a sign error.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":152,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A square matrix cannot have two different inverses.\nReason (R): If B and C are both inverses of a square matrix A, then $B = BI = B(AC) = (BA)C = IC = C$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: A is true. Theorem 3 (uniqueness of inverse) states that the inverse of a square matrix, if it exists, is unique.\nStep 2: R is true. Each step is valid:\n- C is an inverse, so I = AC.\n- Matrix multiplication is associative.\n- B is an inverse, so BA = I.\nStep 3: R shows that any two inverses must coincide. This is exactly the proof of A, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":153,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (number of elements in a matrix) with List-II (number of possible orders).\nList-I:\n(a) 17\n(b) 16\n(c) 36\n(d) 12\nList-II:\n(i) 6\n(ii) 9\n(iii) 2\n(iv) 5","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The number of orders equals the number of divisors $m$ of the element count, since $n = \\frac{\\text{count}}{m}$.\n(a) 17 is prime, with divisors 1 and 17, giving 2 orders → (iii).\n(b) 16 has divisors 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, giving 5 orders → (iv).\n(c) 36 has divisors 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18, 36, giving 9 orders → (ii).\n(d) 12 has divisors 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12, giving 6 orders → (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":154,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A manufacturer sells products x, y and z in two markets. The annual sales (in units) are given by the matrix\n$S = \\begin{bmatrix} 4000 & 1500 & 6000 \\\\ 3000 & 5000 & 2000 \\end{bmatrix}$ (row 1: Market I, row 2: Market II).\nThe unit selling prices of x, y and z are ₹5, ₹8 and ₹2.50. The unit costs are ₹3, ₹6 and ₹1.50 respectively. Using matrix multiplication, the total gross profit from both markets is","image":"","options":["₹35,000","₹18,000","₹1,07,000","₹72,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Profit per unit = price − cost, which gives the column (2, 2, 1).\nS times the profit column gives Market I: $4000(2) + 1500(2) + 6000(1) = 17000$.\nMarket II: $3000(2) + 5000(2) + 2000(1) = 18000$.\nTotal profit = $17000 + 18000 = 35000$.\n₹1,07,000 is the total revenue and ₹72,000 is the total cost.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":155,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Matrices $X$ and $Y$ satisfy $2X + 3Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 7 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 12 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $3X + 2Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 8 & 7 \\\\ 11 & 13 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nStatement I: $X + Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$\nStatement II: $X - Y = \\begin{bmatrix} -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Adding the two equations gives $5(X+Y) = \\begin{bmatrix} 15 & 15 \\\\ 20 & 25 \\end{bmatrix}$. So $X + Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$ and Statement I is correct.\nSubtracting the first from the second gives $X - Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$. Statement II has the opposite sign, so it is incorrect.\nCheck: $X = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $Y = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$ satisfy both given equations.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":156,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $X = \\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfy $X\\begin{bmatrix}1&2\\\\3&5\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1&-2\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2&5\\\\2&4\\end{bmatrix}$. The value of $a+b+c+d$ is","image":"","options":["2","4","0","-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Compute the right side.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1&-2\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2&5\\\\2&4\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2-4 & 5-8\\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2&-3\\\\2&4\\end{bmatrix}$.\nStep 2: Compute the left side.\n$X\\begin{bmatrix}1&2\\\\3&5\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}a+3b & 2a+5b\\\\ c+3d & 2c+5d\\end{bmatrix}$.\nStep 3: Equate the first rows: $a+3b=-2$ and $2a+5b=-3$. Solving gives $b=-1$, $a=1$.\nStep 4: Equate the second rows: $c+3d=2$ and $2c+5d=4$. Solving gives $d=0$, $c=2$.\nStep 5: So $a+b+c+d = 1-1+2+0 = 2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":157,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let A, B and C be square matrices of the same order and k be a scalar. How many of the following statements are always true?\n(i) $(AB)' = A'B'$\n(ii) $(A + B)' = A' + B'$\n(iii) If AB = O, then A = O or B = O\n(iv) $(kA)' = kA'$\n(v) $A(B + C) = AB + AC$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) False: the correct rule is (AB)′ = B′A′, and matrix multiplication is not commutative.\n(ii) True: this is a standard property of transpose.\n(iii) False: for example, $\\begin{bmatrix}0&-1\\\\0&2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3&5\\\\0&0\\end{bmatrix} = O$, although neither factor is O.\n(iv) True: this is a standard property of transpose.\n(v) True: this is the distributive law.\nSo 3 statements are always true.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":158,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\beta\\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $A^2=I$. The value of $\\beta$ is","image":"","options":["$1$","$-1$","$\\frac{5}{3}$","$-\\frac{5}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Compute $A^2$: $A^2=\\begin{bmatrix}4-3\\beta & 2\\beta-2\\beta\\\\ -6+6 & -3\\beta+4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4-3\\beta&0\\\\0&4-3\\beta\\end{bmatrix}$.\nThe off-diagonal entries are always 0.\nFor $A^2=I$ we need $4-3\\beta=1$, so $\\beta=1$.\nThe value $\\frac{5}{3}$ would come from wrongly equating $4-3\\beta$ to $-1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":159,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let A, B and C be matrices of orders $2 \\times 3$, $3 \\times 4$ and $4 \\times 2$ respectively. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) AB\n(B) BC\n(C) CA\n(D) ABC\nList-II:\n(P) $2 \\times 2$\n(Q) $3 \\times 2$\n(R) $2 \\times 4$\n(S) $4 \\times 3$","image":"","options":["A-R, B-Q, C-S, D-P","A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P","A-P, B-Q, C-S, D-R","A-S, B-Q, C-R, D-P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Rule: $(m \\times n)(n \\times p)$ gives a matrix of order $m \\times p$.\nAB: $(2 \\times 3)(3 \\times 4)$ gives $2 \\times 4$ → R.\nBC: $(3 \\times 4)(4 \\times 2)$ gives $3 \\times 2$ → Q.\nCA: $(4 \\times 2)(2 \\times 3)$ gives $4 \\times 3$ → S.\nABC: $(2 \\times 4)(4 \\times 2)$ gives $2 \\times 2$ → P.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":160,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A matrix contains exactly 36 elements. How many different orders $m \\times n$ (with $m, n \\in N$) can it have?","image":"","options":["9","8","5","10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"An $m \\times n$ matrix has $mn$ elements, so we need every ordered pair of natural numbers with $mn = 36$.\nThe divisors of 36 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18 and 36, which is nine divisors.\nEach divisor $m$ fixes $n = 36/m$. So the orders are $1\\times36$, $2\\times18$, $3\\times12$, $4\\times9$, $6\\times6$, $9\\times4$, $12\\times3$, $18\\times2$ and $36\\times1$.\n$6\\times6$ is counted only once, and $4\\times9$ and $9\\times4$ are different orders. That gives 9 orders.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":161,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A $3 \\times 2$ matrix cannot have an inverse.\nReason (R): The inverse of a square matrix, if it exists, is unique.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. For AB = BA = I, both products must be defined and of equal order, which forces A and B to be square matrices of the same order. A rectangular matrix therefore has no inverse.\nR is true. This is NCERT Theorem 3: if B and C are both inverses of A, then B = BI = B(AC) = (BA)C = C.\nR concerns uniqueness, not why rectangular matrices have no inverse, so R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":162,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A matrix has 72 elements, and its number of rows is strictly greater than its number of columns. How many different orders can this matrix have?","image":"","options":["12","6","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"We need $m \\times n$ with $mn = 72$ and $m > n$.\n72 has 12 divisors: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12, 18, 24, 36, 72, so there are 12 ordered pairs in total.\n72 is not a perfect square, so no pair has $m = n$. The pairs split evenly into $m > n$ and $m < n$.\nThat gives $\\frac{12}{2} = 6$ orders: 72×1, 36×2, 24×3, 18×4, 12×6 and 9×8.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":163,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$. Let S be the sum of all the elements of the $3 \\times 3$ matrix AB, and let $BA = [t]$. The value of $S - t$ is","image":"","options":["18","0","42","12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"AB is $3 \\times 3$ with $(i,k)$th element $a_i b_k$.\nThe sum of all elements is therefore $(a_1+a_2+a_3)(b_1+b_2+b_3) = (6)(5) = 30$. So S = 30.\nBA is $1 \\times 1$: $t = 2(1) + (-1)(2) + 4(3) = 12$.\nSo $S - t = 30 - 12 = 18$.\nThe option 0 reflects the false belief that AB and BA carry the same information. In fact they even have different orders.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":164,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The identity matrix $I_n$ is its own inverse.\n(ii) The zero matrix of order 2 is invertible.\n(iii) If A is invertible, then $(A^{-1})^{-1} = A$.\n(iv) A $3 \\times 2$ matrix can have an inverse.\n(v) If A and B are invertible matrices of the same order, then $(AB)^{-1} = A^{-1}B^{-1}$ always.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $I \\cdot I = I$, so $I^{-1} = I$.\n(ii) Incorrect: OB = O ≠ I for every B, so O has no inverse.\n(iii) Correct: $AA^{-1} = A^{-1}A = I$, so A is the inverse of $A^{-1}$ (mutual inverses).\n(iv) Incorrect: a rectangular matrix has no inverse.\n(v) Incorrect: the correct rule is $(AB)^{-1} = B^{-1}A^{-1}$, and the two forms differ in general.\nSo 2 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":165,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (property of a square matrix $A$) with List-II (value of $(I+A)^2$).\nList-I:\n(a) $A^2=A$\n(b) $A^2=I$\n(c) $A^2=O$\n(d) $A^2=-I$\nList-II:\n(i) $2A$\n(ii) $I+3A$\n(iii) $I+2A$\n(iv) $2I+2A$","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In every case, $(I+A)^2=I+2A+A^2$.\n(a) $A^2=A$ gives $I+3A$, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) $A^2=I$ gives $2I+2A$, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) $A^2=O$ gives $I+2A$, so (c)-(iii).\n(d) $A^2=-I$ gives $I+2A-I=2A$, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":166,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$, $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$ and the matrix $X$ satisfies $2A + 3X = 5B$, then the sum of all the elements of $X$ is","image":"","options":["4","$\\frac{8}{3}$","2","$\\frac{16}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $2A + 3X = 5B$.\nAdd $-2A$ to both sides and use the additive inverse and identity: $3X = 5B - 2A$.\nSubstituting: $5B - 2A = \\begin{bmatrix} 10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 15 \\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 6 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 8 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 9 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nSo $X = \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix} 8 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 9 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nSum of the elements $= \\frac{8 - 13 + 4 + 9}{3} = \\frac{8}{3}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":167,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Two farmers P and Q sell three varieties of rice: Basmati, Permal and Naura (columns, in that order). Their sales (in ₹) are given below, with row 1 for P and row 2 for Q.\nSeptember: $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 8000 & 12000 & 6000 \\\\ 15000 & 10000 & 9000 \\end{bmatrix}$\nOctober: $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 5000 & 9000 & 4000 \\\\ 12000 & 7000 & 8000 \\end{bmatrix}$\nUsing $A - B$, the decrease in Permal sales of farmer Q from September to October is","image":"","options":["₹ 2000","₹ 3000","₹ 5000","₹ 17000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The decrease matrix is $A - B = \\begin{bmatrix} 3000 & 3000 & 2000 \\\\ 3000 & 3000 & 1000 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nFarmer Q is row 2 and Permal is column 2. That entry is $10000 - 7000 = 3000$.\nThe option ₹ 17000 adds the sales instead of subtracting them.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":168,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If A and B are diagonal matrices of the same order, then AB = BA.\nReason (R): Matrix multiplication is commutative.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Both products of diagonal matrices are diagonal. The $(i,i)$ entry of each product is $a_{ii}b_{ii}$, and real numbers commute.\nR is false. NCERT Example 14 shows two $2 \\times 2$ matrices with $AB \\neq BA$, so matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\nDiagonal matrices are a special case where the products happen to agree.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":169,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2&3\\\\1&-1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $A^2 = pA + qI$, where I is the identity matrix of order 2, then the value of p + q is","image":"","options":["4","-4","6","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Compute $A^2$:\n$A^2 = \\begin{bmatrix}4+3 & 6-3\\\\2-1 & 3+1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}7&3\\\\1&4\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWrite out the right side: $pA + qI = \\begin{bmatrix}2p+q & 3p\\\\p & -p+q\\end{bmatrix}$.\nEquate corresponding entries. From the (1,2) entries, 3p = 3, so p = 1. From the (1,1) entries, 2 + q = 7, so q = 5.\nCheck the (2,2) entries: -1 + 5 = 4, which matches.\nSo p + q = 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":170,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"A matrix has 12 elements. How many different orders can it have?","image":"","options":["4","5","6","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: the matrix has 12 elements.\nFormula: an $m \\times n$ matrix has $mn$ elements, so we need ordered pairs of natural numbers with $mn = 12$.\nThe pairs are (1,12), (12,1), (2,6), (6,2), (3,4), (4,3).\nThe order is an ordered pair, so $3 \\times 4$ and $4 \\times 3$ are counted separately.\nSo the number of possible orders is 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":171,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let A, B, C be square matrices of the same order. How many of the following are always true?\n(i) $(A'B)' = B'A$\n(ii) $(ABC)' = C'B'A'$\n(iii) $(A^2)' = (A')^2$\n(iv) $(A + B')' = A' + B$\n(v) $(AB)' = A'B'$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) True: $(A'B)' = B'(A')' = B'A$.\n(ii) True: $(ABC)' = C'(AB)' = C'B'A'$.\n(iii) True: $(AA)' = A'A' = (A')^2$.\n(iv) True: $(A + B')' = A' + (B')' = A' + B$.\n(v) False: in general $(AB)' = B'A'$. This equals $A'B'$ only if $A'$ and $B'$ commute.\nSo four statements are always true.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":172,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $A=\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 3 & x \\\\ y & 0 & -5 \\\\ 4 & z & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$ is a skew-symmetric matrix, then the value of $x+y+z$ is:","image":"","options":["$-2$","$2$","$12$","$-12$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For a skew-symmetric matrix, $A' = -A$, so $a_{ji} = -a_{ij}$.\n$x = a_{13} = -a_{31} = -4$.\n$y = a_{21} = -a_{12} = -3$.\n$z = a_{32} = -a_{23} = -(-5) = 5$.\nSo $x+y+z = -4-3+5 = -2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":173,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{bmatrix}$. If $C = AB = [c_{ik}]$, then $c_{21}$ equals","image":"","options":["3","7","2","15"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: $c_{21}$ = (row 2 of A) · (column 1 of B).\nRow 2 of A is [3 4]. Column 1 of B is [1, 0].\nSubstituting: $c_{21} = 3(1) + 4(0) = 3$.\nFor comparison, 7 is $c_{22} = 3(5) + 4(-2)$. It comes from using column 2 of B instead of column 1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":174,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"The product $\\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\end{bmatrix}$ is the $1 \\times 1$ matrix","image":"","options":["[1]","[7]","[-1]","[5]"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A $1 \\times 3$ matrix times a $3 \\times 1$ matrix gives a $1 \\times 1$ matrix.\nMultiply the entries pairwise and add: $1(4) + 2(0) + 3(-1)$.\nThis gives $4 + 0 - 3 = 1$, so the product is [1].\n[7] results from ignoring the negative sign of the last entry.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":175,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Every identity matrix is a scalar matrix.\nReason (R): An identity matrix is a diagonal matrix whose diagonal elements are all equal, each being 1.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A scalar matrix is a diagonal matrix whose diagonal entries are all equal to some constant k.\nR is true: in $I_n$ every off-diagonal entry is 0 and every diagonal entry is 1.\nThis means $I_n$ is a scalar matrix with k = 1, so A is true.\nR states exactly the property that makes A hold, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":176,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"For $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3\\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$, the real number $k$ such that $A^2=kA-2I$ is","image":"","options":["$3$","$-3$","$2$","$1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Compute $A^2$: $A^2=\\begin{bmatrix}16-6 & -12+3\\\\ 8-2 & -6+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10&-9\\\\6&-5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWrite out the right side: $kA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}4k-2 & -3k\\\\ 2k & -k-2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nCompare the (1,2) entries: $-3k=-9$, so $k=3$.\nCheck the other entries: $4(3)-2=10$, $2(3)=6$ and $-3-2=-5$. All agree.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-177","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":177,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$. The matrix $X$ satisfying $A + X = 2B$ is","image":"","options":["$\\begin{bmatrix} 11 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 9 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\end{bmatrix}$","$\\begin{bmatrix} -9 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Add $-A$ (the additive inverse of A) to both sides: $X = 2B - A$.\n$2B = \\begin{bmatrix} 10 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\end{bmatrix}$.\n$2B - A = \\begin{bmatrix} 10-1 & 0-2 \\\\ 4-0 & 2-3 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 9 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1 \\end{bmatrix}$.\nOption (1) is $2B + A$, which comes from a sign slip. Option (4) is $A - 2B$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-178","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":178,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"If $\\begin{bmatrix} a+b & a-b \\\\ 2c+d & c-2d \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$, then the value of $ab + cd$ is:","image":"","options":["7","11","5","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Equate the corresponding entries: a + b = 5, a − b = 1, 2c + d = 7, c − 2d = 1.\nAdding the first two equations: 2a = 6, so a = 3 and b = 2.\nFrom c − 2d = 1, we get c = 1 + 2d.\nSubstituting into 2c + d = 7: 2 + 4d + d = 7, so d = 1 and c = 3.\nSo ab + cd = 6 + 3 = 9.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-179","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":179,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $F(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos x & -\\sin x & 0\\\\ \\sin x & \\cos x & 0\\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nAssertion (A): $F(x)\\,F(-x)=I$ for every real $x$.\nReason (R): $F(x)\\,F(y)=F(x+y)$ for all real $x, y$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: Multiply the matrices. The top-left entry is $\\cos x\\cos y-\\sin x\\sin y=\\cos(x+y)$, and the (1,2) entry is $-(\\sin x\\cos y+\\cos x\\sin y)=-\\sin(x+y)$. The remaining entries match in the same way, so R is true.\nA: Put $y=-x$ in R. Then $F(x)F(-x)=F(0)$.\n$F(0)$ has $\\cos 0=1$ and $\\sin 0=0$, so $F(0)=I$. Hence A is true, and it follows directly from R.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-MATRICES-180","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Matrices","qno":180,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Matrices","type":"mcq","question":"Let $A$ be a skew-symmetric matrix of order $n$, and let $B$ be any square matrix of order $n$.\nStatement I: $B'AB$ is skew-symmetric.\nStatement II: $A^2$ is skew-symmetric.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: $(B'AB)'=B'A'(B')'=B'(-A)B=-(B'AB)$. So $B'AB$ is skew-symmetric. True.\nStatement II: $(A^2)'=(AA)'=A'A'=(-A)(-A)=A^2$. So $A^2$ is symmetric, not skew-symmetric. It could be skew-symmetric only if $A^2=O$, which does not hold in general. False.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 12, Ch 3 · Matrices","difficulty":"medium"}]});
